WEBVTT

00:00:01.700 --> 00:00:06.530
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It's This American Life. I'm Chana Joffe-Walt, sitting in for Ira Glass.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这里是《美国生活》。我是Chana Joffe-Walt，代替Ira Glass主持。

00:00:06.530 --> 00:00:35.580
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>I've been talking to someone here and there over the last couple of months about a situation she's in. And I think she typifies the thing we're going to try to do in this episode, so I'd like you to meet her. Her name is Annika Barber. She's a scientist, a molecular biologist at Rutgers University, a runner. She's got purple hair and a particular way of organizing the world around her-- her books, organized topically and then by height, her lab, color-coded labels.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>过去几个月里，我一直在和某个人断断续续地交流，聊她所处的一种困境。我认为她是我们这期节目想要探讨的问题的典型代表，所以我想请你们认识一下她。她叫Annika Barber，是一位科学家，罗格斯大学的分子生物学家，也是一名跑步爱好者。她染着紫色的头发，对周围的世界有一套独特的整理方式——她的书按主题分类，再按高度排列；她的实验室则用颜色编码标签来管理。

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:42.800
<v Annika Barber>I also track how many days of the year my husband eats pizza, and I make a chart of it. He's really into pizza. So sometimes I just like to have data.
<v Annika Barber>我还记录了我丈夫一年里吃披萨的天数，并做了一张图表。他真的很爱吃披萨。所以我有时就是喜欢掌握数据。

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:45.230
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Wait, wait, wait, why? Why do you do that?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>等等，等等，为什么？你为什么要这样做？

00:00:45.230 --> 00:01:05.960
<v Annika Barber>So my husband really can't go 24 hours without talking about pizza. So last year, I accused him of talking about pizza every day, and he swore he didn't. And I was like, I really think you do. And so starting in January of this year, I said that for 2025, I was going to log every day that I heard him say the word pizza, as well as every time he ate pizza for the year.
<v Annika Barber>因为我丈夫真的无法在24小时内不谈论披萨。去年，我指责他每天都在谈论披萨，但他发誓他没有。我就说，我真的觉得你有。所以从今年一月开始，我说在2025年，我要记录下我听到他说“披萨”这个词的每一天，以及他这一年里吃披萨的每一次。

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:16.790
<v Annika Barber>He actually eats less pizza than I would expect for the amount he talks about pizza. Like, the consumption to discussion ratio is not what I expected it would be. He's only had pizza 14 times this year.
<v Annika Barber>事实上，他吃披萨的频率比我预想的要低，考虑到他谈论披萨的次数。也就是说，消费和讨论的比例并不像我预期的那样。他今年只吃了14次披萨。

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:30.440
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Lately, Annika has been trying to get her mind around a new situation that is deeply confusing for her and lots of people. She's tracking scientific meetings that are not happening.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>最近，Annika一直在试图理解一种让她和许多人都感到非常困惑的新情况。她在追踪那些本该举行却没有举行的科学会议。

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:55.250
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>When President Trump was inaugurated, he put a freeze on research grants. This meant the meetings where scientists like Annika get together at the National Institutes of Health to assess new research-- they're called NIH study sections-- those meetings were off. But then, a judge said the administration couldn't pause all research grants, so the meetings were theoretically back on.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>特朗普总统就职时，他冻结了研究经费。这意味着像Annika这样的科学家在国立卫生研究院（NIH）评估新研究的会议——也就是所谓的“NIH研究组会议”——都被取消了。但后来，一位法官裁定政府不能暂停所有研究经费，所以这些会议理论上又恢复了。

00:01:55.250 --> 00:02:15.170
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>They were listed on an NIH website as scheduled, but Annika says they did not seem to be happening. The website showed they were, but scientists were saying they weren't. Scientists who had submitted grants literally had no idea if their grant had been reviewed and moved on to the next step of the funding process.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>它们在NIH网站上被列为“已安排”，但Annika说这些会议似乎并没有召开。网站显示会议在进行，但科学家们却说没有。那些提交了申请书的科学家们完全不知道他们的项目是否经过了审查，以及是否进入了资助流程的下一步。

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:23.790
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>But Annika learned there was a place you could get a way better picture of whether a meeting was going to happen or had already happened.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>但Annika得知，有一个地方可以让你更清楚地了解会议是否即将举行或已经举行。

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:30.930
<v Annika Barber>I had never even heard of the federal register in my American tax-paying, voting life.
<v Annika Barber>作为一个纳税和投票的美国公民，我以前甚至从未听说过《联邦公报》。

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:32.810
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>What is the federal register?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>什么是《联邦公报》？

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:46.830
<v Annika Barber>The federal register is where all notices of open public meetings that may be happening in the federal government are required to be posted. And for some reason, NIH study sections do have to be posted to the federal register at least 15 days before they meet.
<v Annika Barber>《联邦公报》是联邦政府所有可能召开的公开会议通知必须发布的地方。出于某种原因，NIH的研究组会议必须在会议召开前至少15天在《联邦公报》上发布。

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:52.330
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And if they're not posted to the federal register, they cannot meet.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>如果它们没有发布在《联邦公报》上，它们就不能召开。

00:02:52.330 --> 00:03:07.630
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It's a strange feeling knowing you're in the middle of an upending, knowing that something fundamental to the way that you live is changing, but you can't understand or see the scale of change as it's happening. You just can't. You have to wait.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>当你意识到自己正处于一种剧变的中心，意识到你生活方式中的某些根本性东西正在改变，但你却无法理解或看清这种变化在发生时的规模，这是一种很奇怪的感觉。你就是做不到。你只能等待。

00:03:07.630 --> 00:03:32.230
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>What I like about Annika is she rejects this. She is not waiting. Annika immediately begins scraping data from federal websites, finding all the meetings that were supposed to happen, cross-referencing them with the federal register, reaching out to scientists to see if the supposedly-scheduled meetings did, in fact, occur, compiled all that into a Google spreadsheet, and posted it online.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我喜欢Annika的一点是，她拒绝等待。她没有坐以待毙。Annika立即开始从联邦网站上抓取数据，找出所有本应举行的会议，将它们与《联邦公报》进行交叉核对，联系科学家们查看这些所谓的已安排会议是否真的召开了，并将所有这些汇总到一个谷歌表格中，发布在网上。

00:03:32.230 --> 00:03:48.470
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Anxious and confused scientists all over America began consulting Annika's spreadsheet and passing it around to see is my study section actually happening? Has my grant been reviewed? Is there any point in submitting my next grant? Will I be able to keep my lab open?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>全美各地焦虑而困惑的科学家们开始查阅Annika的表格，并相互传阅，看看：我的研究组会议真的在举行吗？我的申请书被审查了吗？提交下一份申请书还有意义吗？我还能维持我的实验室运作吗？

00:03:48.470 --> 00:03:56.180
<v Annika Barber>There are tens of thousands of scientists whose grants depend on this happening who are all wondering what's going on?
<v Annika Barber>有数以万计的科学家，他们的研究经费取决于这些会议是否举行，他们都在想：到底发生了什么？

00:03:56.180 --> 00:03:59.380
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>How much science has not happened?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>有多少科学研究因此停滞了？

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:09.740
<v Annika Barber>So let's see. Right now, we are at 182 meetings that have not happened.
<v Annika Barber>让我看看。目前，已经有182场会议没有举行。

00:04:09.740 --> 00:04:13.750
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>182 meetings. How many grants is that?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>182场会议。那相当于多少份研究申请？

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:24.800
<v Annika Barber>You know, if we assume roughly 100 grants would have gotten reviewed at each meeting, we're talking about at least 18,000 grants that didn't get reviewed.
<v Annika Barber>你知道，如果我们假设每场会议大约会审查100份申请书，那么至少有18,000份申请书没有得到审查。

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:26.140
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Wow.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>哇。

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:35.990
<v Annika Barber>And that's 18,000 grants across all kinds of things-- I mean, very basic science. Just scrolling through here, I've got things like adaptive immunity and bacterial virulence.
<v Annika Barber>而且这18,000份申请书涵盖了各种领域——我是说，非常基础的科学。随便扫一眼，我有诸如“适应性免疫”和“细菌毒力”之类的课题。

00:04:35.990 --> 00:04:55.210
<v Annika Barber>But then we also have things like addiction risk, and learning and memory and decision making, and therapeutic immune regulation. We have training neuroscientists and physician scientists. And we have studies looking at dentistry and eye diseases, and those aren't meeting.
<v Annika Barber>但我们也看到了一些课题，比如成瘾风险、学习与记忆、决策制定，以及治疗性免疫调节。我们还有正在培养的神经科学家和医生科学家。还有关于牙科和眼科疾病的研究，这些会议都没有召开。

00:04:55.210 --> 00:05:13.300
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>For five weeks, there were no NIH study sections, till it was 205 study sections that did not meet. All of those proposals sat waiting, and all the ones that came after, stacking up behind them, a long traffic jam of uncertainty and science not happening.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>在五周的时间里，没有任何NIH研究组会议举行，最终有205场研究组会议没能召开。所有的提案都搁置在那里，后面新提交的提案也在堆积，形成了一场关于不确定性和科学停滞的漫长交通堵塞。

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:34.540
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And then, in March, a meeting appeared on the federal register, an NIH study section, and then another, and another. Now, the meetings to assess new research grants seem to have mostly picked back up. Scientists are doing peer reviews, but it's not clear if the government will actually cough up the money.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>然后，在三月份，一场会议出现在《联邦公报》上，这是一个NIH研究组会议，接着是另一场，又一场。现在，评估新研究经费的会议似乎大部分已经恢复。科学家们正在进行同行评审，但尚不清楚政府是否真的会拿出这笔钱。

00:05:34.540 --> 00:05:57.460
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And a few of the grant proposals that were supposed to be reviewed at these meetings, they're being quietly disappeared from the lists. They're not being reviewed at all. They seem to be the grants with DEI words or other thought crimes in them that are no longer allowed. It's unclear if the scientists who submitted these grants even know that their proposals have been dropped.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>而原本应该在这些会议上审查的几份研究提案，正悄悄地从名单中消失。它们根本没有被审查。它们似乎是那些包含DEI（多元、公平与包容）词汇或其他被视为“思想罪”内容的申请书，这些词汇已不再被允许。目前还不清楚这些提交申请的科学家是否知道他们的提案已经被取消了。

00:05:57.460 --> 00:06:04.430
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Annika went to a meeting herself. It was two months late, pulled together at the very last minute, but it did happen.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Annika本人也参加了一场会议。会议迟到了两个月，在最后一刻匆忙拼凑起来，但确实召开了。

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:30.980
<v Annika Barber>We all made the time, and people were there, and people gave the same quality of review that I have always experienced in these meetings, and the same level of scientific discussion. But it felt so weird to be having these scientific discussions in this climate where you could only wonder, like, yeah, we have all these amazing scientists in this room reviewing the future of the next five years of research in the areas of molecular neurogenetics.
<v Annika Barber>我们都腾出了时间，人们都到场了，大家进行的审查质量和我在这类会议上一贯经历的一样，科学讨论的水平也一样。但在这种氛围下进行这些科学讨论感觉非常奇怪，你只能纳闷，是的，我们房间里有这么多优秀的科学家在审查分子神经遗传学领域未来五年的研究方向。

00:06:30.980 --> 00:06:38.210
<v Annika Barber>But is any of this going to be funded? Are any of these studies going to go forward? Is the NIH going to give these people any money?
<v Annika Barber>但这些研究能得到资助吗？这些研究能推进吗？NIH会给这些人钱吗？

00:06:38.210 --> 00:06:41.360
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Did anybody ask? Did anybody ask?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>有人问过吗？有人问过吗？

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:50.300
<v Annika Barber>No, no. Every grant gets 15 minutes for discussion, and we needed to get through 50-odd grants, so there's no time to think about anything else.
<v Annika Barber>没有，没有。每份申请书只有15分钟的讨论时间，我们需要审阅50多份申请书，所以根本没时间去想别的。

00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:55.930
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So will that stuff that you reviewed get funded?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>那你审阅的那些东西会得到资助吗？

00:06:55.930 --> 00:07:00.820
<v Annika Barber>I don't know.
<v Annika Barber>我不知道。

00:07:00.820 --> 00:07:05.930
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Was there a part of you that felt like, are we chumps? Are we just like--
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>你内心有没有一种感觉，觉得我们是不是傻瓜？我们是不是只是……

00:07:05.930 --> 00:07:18.110
<v Annika Barber>Yes, absolutely. It feels like we're fiddling while Rome burns, talking about some cool genes, and worms, and mice, and all the interesting genome-wide association studies that we could do if money keeps existing.
<v Annika Barber>是的，绝对是。感觉就像罗马在燃烧，我们却在拉小提琴一样，讨论着一些很酷的基因、蠕虫、老鼠，以及如果钱还存在的话我们本可以做的所有有趣的基因组关联研究。

00:07:18.110 --> 00:07:19.100
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Uh huh.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>嗯哼。

00:07:19.100 --> 00:07:30.520
<v Annika Barber>And yeah, are we chumps? Are we participating in furthering something or nothing? Are we just wasting our time?
<v Annika Barber>是的，我们是傻瓜吗？我们是在助长某种东西还是什么都没有？我们是不是在浪费时间？

00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:48.480
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>The thing that is killing her is not knowing the shape of the new normal. How many grants will now be funded compared to before-- half, a quarter? Which scientists will get funding? What kind of research? There's no data she can turn to that will tell her that.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>让她感到痛苦的不是不知道“新常态”的形状。与以前相比，现在有多少研究会被资助——一半，四分之一？哪些科学家会获得资助？什么样的研究？没有任何数据能告诉她这些。

00:07:48.480 --> 00:08:03.960
<v Annika Barber>And so I need the data of how bad is it? I think a lot of us are used to approaching problems as something that we can, not necessarily solve with data, but that we can find the edges of with data--
<v Annika Barber>所以我需要数据来了解情况到底有多糟糕？我想我们很多人习惯于把问题当作我们可以解决的东西，不一定是通过数据解决，但我们可以通过数据找到问题的边缘——

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:04.650
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Mm-hmm.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>嗯哼。

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:12.100
<v Annika Barber>--and get our arms around the problem, and define the problem by figuring out what the data are and what the--
<v Annika Barber>——掌握问题的全貌，并通过找出数据是什么来定义问题，以及……

00:08:12.100 --> 00:08:17.680
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>But it might be possible that that's not going to happen in this case, that there might not be--
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>但有可能在这一案例中，这种情况不会发生，可能根本就不会有……

00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:35.820
<v Annika Barber>That does say-- yeah, I'm starting to get that vibe. Yeah, it becomes one of those things where can you graph chaos? Maybe not. Can you feel better trying to graph chaos? Also maybe not.
<v Annika Barber>确实说得对——是的，我开始感觉到这种氛围了。是的，它变成了一种“你能为混乱绘制图表吗”的问题？也许不能。尝试为混乱绘制图表能让你感觉好点吗？可能也不会。

00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:40.980
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Will she continued to try to graph the chaos? Yes, she will.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>她会继续尝试为混乱绘制图表吗？是的，她会的。

00:08:40.980 --> 00:09:09.510
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Today's show is full of people who do not wait for the chaos to settle, people who run toward it, who will take whatever little data they have available to them and try to make it make sense. These are people who get answers. Stay with us.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>今天的节目充满了那些不愿等待混乱平息的人，那些冲向混乱的人，他们会利用手中现有的微薄数据，试图让一切变得合理。这些人总能找到答案。请留步。

00:09:09.510 --> 00:09:21.180
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It's This American Life. The questions people are trying to answer in today's episode, they are basic-- where and why? Act One-- Solve for Why.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这里是《美国生活》。今天节目中人们试图回答的问题非常基本——在哪里？为什么？第一幕——求解“为什么”。

00:09:21.180 --> 00:09:45.420
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>One of the hardest places to see through chaos in the middle of a war-- fog of war, all that. This is especially true for the war in Gaza. There is very limited information moving in and out of Gaza. Israel has banned international press from entering the strip for nearly 18 months, except for a few brief trips, accompanied by and under the control of the Israeli military.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>战争中很难看清混乱——战争迷雾之类的。这对加沙战争来说尤其如此。进出加沙的信息非常有限。以色列已禁止国际媒体进入加沙地带近18个月，除了少数几次由以色列军方陪同并处于其控制之下的短暂行程。

00:09:45.420 --> 00:10:05.520
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>One rare outside group has gotten a view on the ground of Gaza-- medical workers. Since the start of the war, over 100 American doctors and nurses have traveled to Gaza, treated patients there for weeks at a time, and come back out. Producer Ike Sriskandarajah talked to a dozen of them who volunteered there.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>有一个罕见的外部群体在加沙实地有所见闻——医务工作者。自战争爆发以来，已有超过100名美国医生和护士前往加沙，在那里治疗伤者，每次待上几周，然后撤出。制作人Ike Sriskandarajah采访了十几位曾在那里志愿服务的医生。

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:38.550
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Dr. Mark Perlmutter had been on about 40 medical missions all over the developing world before he went to Gaza. He's a white guy, almost 70, a hand surgeon in rural North Carolina when he's not doing humanitarian work. And when he found out patients in Gaza needed an orthopedic specialist like himself, he signed up and flew there in March of 2024. He'd worked in some pretty violent places, but never an active war zone, and only knew the basics about the conflict between Israelis and Palestinians.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Mark Perlmutter医生在去加沙之前，已经在全球发展中国家参加过约40次医疗任务。他是一位白人，快70岁了，在不进行人道主义工作时，他是北卡罗来纳州农村的一名手外科医生。当他得知加沙的伤者需要像他这样的骨科专家时，他报名并于2024年3月飞往那里。他曾在一些非常动荡的地方工作过，但从未在活跃的战区工作过，对以色列人和巴勒斯坦人之间的冲突也只了解皮毛。

00:10:38.550 --> 00:10:57.730
<v Mark Perlmutter>I went there, only because my twist is to operate on kids who are hurt. And I knew that Gaza was 50% kids, and that's all I knew. I thought "infitada" was a type of Mexican dish. And, I mean, I knew nothing.
<v Mark Perlmutter>我去那里仅仅是因为我的特长是为受伤的孩子做手术。我知道加沙有50%的孩子，我只知道这些。我当时以为“起义”（Intifada）是一种墨西哥菜。我是说，我什么都不知道。

00:10:57.730 --> 00:11:08.260
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Intifada is the word for Palestinian uprising against Israeli occupation. But Mark was about to get a crash course from one of his fellow doctors.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>“起义”是指巴勒斯坦人为反抗以色列占领而进行的起义。但Mark很快就要从他的一位同行医生那里上一门速成课了。

00:11:08.260 --> 00:11:35.620
<v Mark Perlmutter>We got on the van, and I heard Feroze talking casually to somebody else. He's got this deep baritone voice. And, you know, you meet somebody for the first time, and within minutes you can tell that somebody is a whole lot smarter than you. And my first message was, there's absolutely no way that I'm going to get on this van and not sit next to this dude, or behind him, or within earshot of him.
<v Mark Perlmutter>我们上了车，我听到Feroze在和别人随意交谈。他的声音深沉，像男中音。你知道，当你第一次见到某人，几分钟内你就能看出对方比你聪明得多。我当时的第一想法是，我绝对不能上这辆车却不坐在他旁边，或者他后面，或者在他的听力范围内。

00:11:35.620 --> 00:11:53.160
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Dr. Feroze Sidhwa, a trauma surgeon from the US-- he's also volunteered as a doctor in the war in Ukraine and with Palestinians in the West Bank. He's closely studied the Israeli-Palestinian conflict, though his family is from a small ethnic minority in what is now Pakistan.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Feroze Sidhwa医生，来自美国的创伤外科医生——他此前也曾在乌克兰战争中以及在西岸的巴勒斯坦人中担任志愿医生。他密切研究过以巴冲突，尽管他的家人来自现在巴基斯坦境内的一个少数民族。

00:11:53.160 --> 00:12:09.900
<v Feroze Sidhwa>When people look at me, they assume I'm Palestinian or something, but I'm not. I lived in Haifa. That was from 2004 to '05, and I worked with an Arab Jewish cooperative that doesn't exist anymore. And during that time, I toured around in the West Bank-- was never able to go to Gaza, but toured around in the West Bank a lot.
<v Feroze Sidhwa>当人们看着我时，他们以为我是巴勒斯坦人或其他什么，但我不是。我曾在海法住过。那是2004年到2005年，我曾与一个已经不存在的阿拉伯-犹太人合作组织一起工作。在那段时间里，我在西岸到处走访——虽然从未能去加沙，但在西岸转了很多地方。

00:12:09.900 --> 00:12:21.260
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>So Mark sat next to Feroze, and when they arrived at the European Hospital-- that's the name of the hospital in Khan Younis in Gaza-- their team dropped off their thousands of pounds of supplies.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>于是Mark坐在了Feroze旁边，当他们到达欧洲医院——那是汗尤尼斯加沙的一家医院的名字——时，他们的团队卸下了数千磅的物资。

00:12:21.260 --> 00:12:44.610
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>European Hospital was built for just over 200 beds, but is taking care of over 1,000 people, and families are living in every corner of the hospital. Mark hadn't seen anything like it. Feroze hadn't either, but he told me he didn't linger on that long. A nurse took them on a quick tour, let them know where everything was-- the operating room, the ICU.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>欧洲医院原本只有200多张床位，但现在却要照顾1,000多人，家庭住在医院的每一个角落。Mark从未见过这样的景象。Feroze也没见过，但他告诉我他没有在那上面停留太久。一名护士带他们快速参观了一下，告诉他们东西都在哪——手术室、ICU。

00:12:44.610 --> 00:12:55.400
<v Feroze Sidhwa>The nurse that was showing us around didn't really speak English very well, and she just pointed at these two kids, and just pointed at her head, and said, shot, shot. There were four kids in the hospital with gunshot wounds to the head.
<v Feroze Sidhwa>带我们参观的护士英语说得不是很好，她指着两个孩子，指了指自己的头，说：“枪击，枪击。”医院里有四个头部中枪的孩子。

00:12:55.400 --> 00:13:03.360
<v Feroze Sidhwa>I just thought that that was unbelievable. And I just assumed that she was just wrong. I didn't think she was lying, but she was just incorrect. That probably was a shrapnel injury or something like that.
<v Feroze Sidhwa>我当时觉得这太不可思议了。我只是以为她弄错了。我不认为她在撒谎，只是她不准确。那可能只是弹片伤之类的。

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:16.140
<v Feroze Sidhwa>But then, I looked at these kids, and they didn't have any other evidence of an explosive injury. And then we pulled up their CT scans, and sure enough, it did look like they had been shot in the head. And then we went on and found two more kids also shot in the head in the other ICUs.
<v Feroze Sidhwa>但后来，我看了这些孩子，他们身上没有其他爆炸伤的迹象。然后我们调出了他们的CT扫描，结果确实看起来像是头部中弹了。然后我们继续走，在其他ICU里又发现了两个头部中枪的孩子。

00:13:16.140 --> 00:13:25.230
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>This is, like, minutes into your initial tour of the hospital. You see two kids with gunshots to the head and then two more kids with gunshots to the head.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>这是你刚开始参观医院的几分钟内，你就看到了两个头部中枪的孩子，然后又看到了两个头部中枪的孩子。

00:13:25.230 --> 00:13:35.370
<v Feroze Sidhwa>Mm-hmm. Yeah, for me, what struck me about it was just the fact that it had happened. I work in a pretty rough part of the country, so I do see children who get shot in the head.
<v Feroze Sidhwa>嗯哼。是的，对我来说，让我震惊的是这件事居然发生了。我在这个国家相当糟糕的一个地区工作，所以我确实见过头部中弹的孩子。

00:13:35.370 --> 00:13:41.930
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Feroze works at a hospital near Stockton, California, which has higher rates of violent crime than most of the country.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Feroze在加州斯托克顿附近的一家医院工作，那里的暴力犯罪率比全国大部分地区都要高。

00:13:41.930 --> 00:13:51.540
<v Feroze Sidhwa>But to see four kids with gunshot wounds to the head already admitted to the hospital when I get there, it certainly struck me as being very unusual.
<v Feroze Sidhwa>但看到四个头部中弹的孩子在我到达时就已经被收治在医院里，这确实让我觉得非常罕见。

00:13:51.540 --> 00:14:11.090
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>I mean, it was a war zone, but still, this was a lot. And then, he moved on. There were so many people that needed medical attention. The only way he could keep track of his patients was to make notes on his iPhone. Just to warn you here, the details are graphic.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>我是说，那是一个战区，但这仍然太多了。然后，他继续工作。有太多人需要医疗照顾。他记录病人情况的唯一方法是在iPhone上做笔记。这里先提醒一下，细节很残酷。

00:14:11.090 --> 00:14:14.790
<v Feroze Sidhwa>Here, this is from March 28.
<v Feroze Sidhwa>这是3月28日的记录。

00:14:14.790 --> 00:14:19.730
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>He pulled up an entry from a random day, just to give me a sense of what it was like.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>他调出了一天随意的条目，只是为了让我了解当时的情况。

00:14:19.730 --> 00:14:31.050
<v Feroze Sidhwa>Overnight, we had another mass casualty event. EMS told us a building with a large number of people in it was bombed. And then, two children arrived dead. The one I saw was 12 months old, and the other was reportedly two years old.
<v Feroze Sidhwa>隔夜，我们又发生了一次大规模伤亡事件。急救人员告诉我们，一栋住着大量人的建筑被炸毁了。然后，两个孩子被送来时已经死了。我看到的那个只有12个月大，另一个据报道是两岁。

00:14:31.050 --> 00:14:48.600
<v Feroze Sidhwa>Next bullet-- I took care of a seven-year-old girl with a blast injury pneumothorax to the right lung and a bad contusion to the left lung. Next bullet-- I took care of a three-year-old child who had a massive aspiration after a head injury. Next bullet-- I took care of a 20-year-old woman with a left hemopneumothorax and depressed open skull fracture.
<v Feroze Sidhwa>下一条——我救治了一个7岁的小女孩，右肺有爆炸伤导致的气胸，左肺有严重的挫伤。下一条——我救治了一个3岁的小孩，头部受伤后发生了严重误吸。下一条——我救治了一个20岁的妇女，左侧血气胸和凹陷性开放性颅骨骨折。

00:14:48.600 --> 00:14:59.130
<v Feroze Sidhwa>Next bullet-- I took care of her sister. She had almost identical injuries. Next bullet-- finally, I took care of a 25-year-old man with a frontal skull fracture and small associated subdural hematoma.
<v Feroze Sidhwa>下一条——我救治了她的姐妹。她有几乎相同的伤势。下一条——最后，我救治了一个25岁的男子，额骨骨折，并伴有小的硬膜下血肿。

00:14:59.130 --> 00:15:01.410
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>So that's, like, an 18-hour day.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>所以那是一个18小时的工作日？

00:15:01.410 --> 00:15:04.200
<v Feroze Sidhwa>No, that was-- all of that probably happened in one hour.
<v Feroze Sidhwa>不，那是……所有这些可能发生在一个小时内。

00:15:04.200 --> 00:15:05.670
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>That's one hour?
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>那是一个小时？

00:15:05.670 --> 00:15:08.690
<v Feroze Sidhwa>That probably took about an hour or an hour and a half. Yeah.
<v Feroze Sidhwa>那可能花了一个小时或一个半小时。是的。

00:15:08.690 --> 00:15:23.250
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>90 minutes-- it's intense. But in that endless stream of traumatic injuries, there's one that jumped out at him every time, kids getting shot in the head. Whenever he saw this, he'd make a note of it on his phone.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>90分钟——这很紧张。但在那连绵不断的创伤病例流中，有一件事每次都让他感到震惊：孩子们头部中枪。每当他看到这一点，他就会在手机上做个笔记。

00:15:23.250 --> 00:15:34.470
<v Feroze Sidhwa>And what I wrote down is that I was going through the ICU, and I found an eight-year-old girl shot in the head overnight. Her pupils are fixed and dilated. It's a transcranial gunshot wound, definitely non-survivable.
<v Feroze Sidhwa>我写下来的是，我正在检查ICU，发现了一个隔夜头部中枪的8岁女孩。她的瞳孔固定并放大。这是贯通颅脑的枪伤，绝对无法存活。

00:15:34.470 --> 00:15:39.900
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>This is an eight-year-old girl with a bullet wound that went through her head.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>这是一个8岁的小女孩，子弹穿过了她的头。

00:15:39.900 --> 00:15:50.550
<v Feroze Sidhwa>Yeah, the bullet didn't stop. And then, let's see, the next day. So the next day, the eight-year-old girl had died, and in the same bed is a 14-year-old boy shot in the right chest and the head.
<v Feroze Sidhwa>是的，子弹没有停下。然后，让我们看看，第二天。所以第二天，那个8岁的小女孩死了，在同一张床上的是一个14岁的男孩，胸部右侧和头部中枪。

00:15:50.550 --> 00:16:04.890
<v Feroze Sidhwa>The next day, I said, I went through the ICU afterwards. The 14-year-old boy turns out to be 12 when his family arrived. So then, let's see, two days later, he's been replaced by a 13-year-old boy shot in the head. I wrote, he'll also die.
<v Feroze Sidhwa>第二天，我说，之后我检查了ICU。那个14岁的男孩在家人到达后，发现他其实是12岁。然后，让我们看看，两天后，他被一个13岁头部中枪的男孩取代了。我写道，他也会死。

00:16:04.890 --> 00:16:20.270
<v Feroze Sidhwa>So then on that same day, I wrote, I took care of a two-year-old girl who was brought to the ED after being shot in the head. She arrived with bilateral fixed and dilated pupils, also a non-survivable brain injury. We then had a mass casualty event a few minutes later.
<v Feroze Sidhwa>所以同一天，我写道，我救治了一个2岁的小女孩，在头部中枪后被送进急诊室。她到达时双侧瞳孔固定放大，也是不可存活的脑损伤。几分钟后我们又发生了一次大规模伤亡事件。

00:16:20.270 --> 00:16:36.010
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>At the same time that Feroze was starting to document this, Mark, working with his patients-- he was seeing the same thing. He vividly remembered the day he saw two kids brought in who had both been shot in the head and the chest.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>在Feroze开始记录这一点时，Mark在救治他的病人时，看到了同样的事情。他清楚地记得有一天他看到两个孩子被送来，他们都头部和胸部中枪。

00:16:36.010 --> 00:16:58.060
<v Mark Perlmutter>One of the kids was there with a family member. I ripped up his shirt, and there was a bullet entry wound right over the heart. And then I picked up the dressings on his forehead, and a second bullet went in right in front of his left ear hole, in front of his ear and out of his neck.
<v Mark Perlmutter>其中一个孩子在一位家人的陪同下。我撕开了他的衬衫，心脏上方有一个子弹入口。然后我揭开了他额头上的敷料，第二颗子弹从他左耳孔前方进去，从耳前进入，从颈部穿出。

00:16:58.060 --> 00:17:02.080
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Oh, my god. What was the kid doing when this happened?
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>天哪。这孩子当时在做什么？

