<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Was there a street battle happening?
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>当时发生了街头交火吗？

00:17:10.040 --> 00:17:15.820
<v Mark Perlmutter>I didn't ask if there was a street battle going on, but it happened twice in the same day.
<v Mark Perlmutter>我没问是不是有街头交火，但这件事在同一天发生了两次。

00:17:15.820 --> 00:17:18.250
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Could you say the second time?
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>能再说一次第二次吗？

00:17:18.250 --> 00:17:35.320
<v Mark Perlmutter>Yeah, right next to that kid was another kid who got shot in the head and the chest. And that child had no adult with him, so I couldn't get a story. It's hard to see it.
<v Mark Perlmutter>是的，就在那孩子旁边是另一个头部和胸部中枪的孩子。那个孩子身边没有大人，所以我无法得知原委。这太难看了。

00:17:35.320 --> 00:17:46.150
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>These weren't kids injured by collapsing buildings. They were kids who'd been shot-- direct gunshot wounds into 12-year-olds, eight-year-olds, even toddlers.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>这些不是被坍塌建筑物压伤的孩子。他们是被枪击的孩子——直接对12岁、8岁甚至幼儿的头部开枪。

00:17:46.150 --> 00:17:53.810
<v Mark Perlmutter>Oh, over a dozen, combined two injuries, only two, head or chest, a dozen others.
<v Mark Perlmutter>哦，十几例，两种伤势合并，只有两例，头部或胸部，其他十几例也是如此。

00:17:53.810 --> 00:17:56.050
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Almost every day you were there?
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>你待在那里的几乎每天都有？

00:17:56.050 --> 00:17:57.970
<v Mark Perlmutter>Yeah.
<v Mark Perlmutter>是的。

00:17:57.970 --> 00:18:07.840
<v Feroze Sidhwa>By the time I left, I had seen 13 children shot in the head total, or at least that's what I recorded in my journal. I probably saw more, but I try to stick to what I actually documented.
<v Feroze Sidhwa>我离开时，总共看到了13个头部中枪的孩子，或者至少是我在日记里记录下来的。我可能看到的更多，但我尽量只记录我实际证实的东西。

00:18:07.840 --> 00:18:19.580
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>13 children in 14 days. Even with all the other traumatic injuries and deaths they saw, the kids who were shot really stuck with Mark. It was haunting him.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>14天里有13个孩子。尽管他们还看到了其他所有创伤和死亡，但那些中枪的孩子真正困扰着Mark。它一直萦绕在他心头。

00:18:19.580 --> 00:18:33.500
<v Mark Perlmutter>Early on, I thought it was just an isolated jerk carrying out, because every army has jerks. War changes people, and so you can absolutely have rogue people behaving inappropriately.
<v Mark Perlmutter>起初，我以为这只是一个孤立的混蛋在执行任务，因为每支军队都有混蛋。战争会改变人，所以你绝对会有那些行为不当的流氓分子。

00:18:33.500 --> 00:18:56.590
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>That's as far as Mark thought about it at the time. The detail of 13 kids, all with gunshot wounds to the head and chest, it just got added to the long list of things they saw in Gaza and couldn't fully understand. It didn't come back to Feroze until they were back home and got invited to speak at a conference in Dearborn, Michigan.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Mark当时的想法仅限于此。13个孩子全部头部和胸部中枪的细节，只是被加到了他们在加沙看到且无法完全理解的事情的长名单中。直到他们回到家，并受邀在密歇根州迪尔伯恩的一次会议上发言，这事儿才回到Feroze的脑海中。

00:18:56.590 --> 00:19:06.130
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>The panel had another American doctor, who also volunteered in Gaza. He worked in two different hospitals a couple months before Feroze and Mark arrived.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>小组讨论还有另一位美国医生，他也曾在加沙志愿服务。他在Feroze和Mark到达前几个月曾在两家不同的医院工作。

00:19:06.130 --> 00:19:20.110
<v Feroze Sidhwa>He's a Palestinian-American in Chicago named Thaer Ahmed. And we were just sitting next to each other, talking before our panel started. And I said, I couldn't believe how many kids I saw shot in the head. I just went through my journal recently, and it was 13.
<v Feroze Sidhwa>他是一位来自芝加哥的巴勒斯坦裔美国人，名叫Thaer Ahmed。在会议开始前，我们坐在一起聊天。我说，我简直不敢相信我看到那么多孩子头部中枪。我最近刚查了我的日记，是13个。

00:19:20.110 --> 00:19:27.280
<v Feroze Sidhwa>And he said, oh, yeah, I know, me, too. Almost every day, I saw a kid shot in the head. And I was like, oh, OK.
<v Feroze Sidhwa>他说，哦，是的，我知道，我也是。几乎每天，我都看到一个孩子头部中枪。我就说，噢，好吧。

00:19:27.280 --> 00:19:35.090
<v Mark Perlmutter>And then Feroze texted me, hey, this guy saw other kids shot. We have to find out who else saw other kids shot.
<v Mark Perlmutter>然后Feroze给我发短信说，嘿，这家伙也看到了其他中枪的孩子。我们得找出还有谁看到了其他中枪的孩子。

00:19:35.090 --> 00:19:41.150
<v Feroze Sidhwa>So I started asking other people who had been over there-- physicians, nurses, midwives, nurse practitioners.
<v Feroze Sidhwa>所以我开始询问其他去过那里的人——医生、护士、助产士、执业护士。

00:19:41.150 --> 00:19:46.730
<v Adam Hamawy>I got a call from Feroze Sidhwa. I think sometime in mid-July, he called me.
<v Adam Hamawy>我接到了Feroze Sidhwa的电话。我想是在7月中旬的某个时候，他打给我的。

00:19:46.730 --> 00:19:50.510
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Dr. Adam Hamawy is a plastic surgeon from New Jersey.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Adam Hamawy医生是来自新泽西州的整形外科医生。

00:19:50.510 --> 00:19:59.270
<v Adam Hamawy>And he said, did you take care of anyone with any gunshot wounds to the head or face? And I said, yes. And he said, really? How many?
<v Adam Hamawy>他说，你救治过任何头部或面部中枪的人吗？我说，是的。他说，真的吗？有多少？

00:19:59.270 --> 00:20:13.690
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Because of Adam's specialty, he would have only seen children who survived a gunshot to the head long enough to make it to reconstructive surgery. Not many did. He saw one. It was still shocking to him.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>由于Adam的专业，他只会见到那些头部中枪后存活时间长到足以进行重建手术的孩子。没几个人能做到。他见到了一个。这对他来说仍然很震惊。

00:20:13.690 --> 00:20:22.840
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Adam had served in Iraq as a surgeon in the US Army. He treated lots of injured people, but what he was seeing in Gaza was different.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Adam曾在伊拉克作为美国陆军的外科医生服役。他救治过许多受伤的人，但在加沙看到的情况却不同。

00:20:22.840 --> 00:20:40.370
<v Adam Hamawy>When I was in Iraq, there were civilians that were injured. There were children that were injured. And that's called incidental, collateral damage, all the terms that we use to cleanly justify what's happening. But the scale was, I mean, not even-- not even close to this.
<v Adam Hamawy>当我在伊拉克时，确实有平民受伤。有孩子受伤。那是所谓的附带伤害，我们用来干净利落地证明正在发生的事情的所有术语。但规模，我是说，甚至——甚至根本无法与此相提并论。

00:20:40.370 --> 00:20:59.740
<v Adam Hamawy>I mean, I probably took care of, like, five, six children the whole time I was in Iraq, and I wasn't there for three weeks. I was there for eight months. I mean, it didn't look-- it didn't appear that they were intentional targets. Those you could really say that they were wrong place, wrong time.
<v Adam Hamawy>我的意思是，我在伊拉克待了八个月，可能救治了五、六个孩子。我的意思是，看起来——并不像是他们是故意目标。那些你确实可以说他们是处于错误的时间、错误的地点。

00:20:59.740 --> 00:21:14.950
<v Adam Hamawy>I didn't see targeted gunshots to little kids that were five, six years old or 10, 15 years old. In fact, I mean, I'm thinking back. I mean, I don't think I saw a gunshot wound to a kid at all when I was there.
<v Adam Hamawy>我没有看到针对5、6岁或10、15岁小孩子的有针对性的枪击。事实上，我是说，我在回想。我的意思是，我不认为我在那里时看到过一个孩子的枪伤。

00:21:14.950 --> 00:21:24.600
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>There were documented cases where Americans did kill Iraqi children, but nothing near the scale that these doctors saw in Gaza.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>有记录在案的案例表明美国人确实杀死了伊拉克儿童，但规模远不及这些医生在加沙看到的情况。

00:21:24.600 --> 00:21:38.460
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>As somebody with US military experience, do you have a perspective that could explain the circumstances that lead to a kid getting shot? How do you answer that?
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>作为有美国军事经验的人，你有什么视角可以解释导致孩子中枪的环境吗？你如何回答这个问题？

00:21:38.460 --> 00:22:06.690
<v Adam Hamawy>These are little children that are being shot, and these aren't stray bullets. These are aimed. They're precise. So a stray bullet will explain one or two of them. It's not going to explain the string of precise, targeted shootings that are being done on children since October.
<v Adam Hamawy>这些都是被枪击的小孩子，而且这些不是流弹。这些是瞄准的。它们很精确。所以流弹可以解释其中的一两起。它无法解释自10月以来针对儿童的一连串精确的、有针对性的枪击事件。

00:22:06.690 --> 00:22:31.020
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>The medical worker I spoke with who spent the most time in Gaza also saw the most kids shot-- 50. She showed me a picture she took of a scan of a five or six-year-old's skull. There's a bullet in the middle of it. She was told this child was playing with their friends when an armed quadcopter drone came overhead and shot the child.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>我采访的一位在加沙待的时间最长的医务人员也看到了最多的中枪儿童——50个。她给我看了她拍摄的一张5或6岁孩子头骨扫描的照片。中间有一颗子弹。她被告知这个孩子在和朋友玩耍时，一架武装四轴飞行器从头顶飞过，并射杀了这个孩子。

00:22:31.020 --> 00:22:52.320
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Dr. Ahlia Kattan also responded to Feroze's callout. She's an anesthesiologist from Southern California. She went with her husband, and between the two of them saw 15 kids who had been shot in the head or chest. The one that really stuck with her also happened to be the youngest case I'd heard of.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Ahlia Kattan医生也回应了Feroze的号召。她是来自南加州的麻醉师。她和丈夫一起去了，他们两人之间总共看到了15个头部或胸部中枪的孩子。那个让她感触最深的孩子也恰好是我听过的最小的病例。

00:22:52.320 --> 00:23:04.440
<v Ahlia Kattan>One day, I got called in for a mass trauma in the emergency room. They said, we need help, so I assumed it was just from bombings. And this 18-month-old got thrown into my hands. Her name was Liliana.
<v Ahlia Kattan>有一天，我被叫去处理急诊室的大规模创伤。他们说，我们需要帮助，所以我以为只是炸弹引起的。这个18个月大的孩子被塞进我的怀里。她的名字叫Liliana。

00:23:04.440 --> 00:23:16.020
<v Ahlia Kattan>I knew she was dead, but her mom was screaming, so I knew we had to do something and show her we tried. So I didn't see any blood frothing out of her mouth, and I was doing chest compressions.
<v Ahlia Kattan>我知道她已经死了，但她的妈妈在尖叫，所以我知道我们必须做些什么，向她展示我们尽力了。所以我没有看到任何血从她嘴里涌出，我正在做心肺复苏。

00:23:16.020 --> 00:23:39.300
<v Ahlia Kattan>And I looked up at her head, and I saw there was blood. And I asked her, was there any imaging? They handed me a CT scan, and there was a bullet shot to her head, and you could tell it was like a shot right to her temple. And I was just, like, in shock to-- never have I been done this many kids, but I've never done chest compressions on a gunshot wound to an 18-month-old.
<v Ahlia Kattan>我抬头看向她的头，我看到有血。我问她，有做过影像检查吗？他们递给我一份CT扫描，那是头部中弹，你可以看出就像是射中了她的太阳穴。我当时简直，惊呆了——我从未见过这么多孩子，但我从未对一个18个月大、头部中弹的孩子进行过心肺复苏。

00:23:39.300 --> 00:23:51.180
<v Ahlia Kattan>And the mom was just screaming. The mom, this was her only daughter, and she had tried for nine years to have her. And she was just hysterical, obviously.
<v Ahlia Kattan>那个妈妈在尖叫。妈妈，这是她唯一的女儿，她试了九年才怀上她。她显然已经崩溃了。

00:23:51.180 --> 00:24:04.440
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Feroze reached out to as many American medical workers as he could-- doctors, nurses, paramedics. He created a survey to send out and compiled all the answers. The results stunned him.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Feroze尽可能多地联系了美国医务工作者——医生、护士、护理人员。他创建了一份调查问卷发出去，并汇总了所有的回答。结果让他惊呆了。

00:24:04.440 --> 00:24:11.370
<v Feroze Sidhwa>Almost everybody had the exact same experience. Almost universally, they said the same thing, which I really was surprised by.
<v Feroze Sidhwa>几乎所有人的经历都完全一样。几乎普遍地，他们说了同样的话，这让我确实感到惊讶。

00:24:11.370 --> 00:24:23.550
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Out of the 53 American medical workers surveyed who did emergency care for children in Gaza, 44 said they saw kids shot in the head or chest. Here's Mark.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>在受访的53名曾为加沙儿童进行紧急护理的美国医务工作者中，44人表示他们看到过孩子头部或胸部中枪。这是Mark的情况。

00:24:23.550 --> 00:24:35.220
<v Mark Perlmutter>83% said they saw a child who was shot in the chest or head-- 83%. So it's not just my finding.
<v Mark Perlmutter>83%的人说他们看到了头部或胸部中枪的孩子——83%。所以这不仅仅是我的发现。

00:24:35.220 --> 00:24:41.130
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Could you describe that moment when you saw just how many?
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>你能描述一下当你看到有多少人时那一刻的感觉吗？

00:24:41.130 --> 00:24:47.910
<v Mark Perlmutter>Oh, I cried. Yeah, because it gave a number to the tragedy.
<v Mark Perlmutter>噢，我哭了。是的，因为这给悲剧赋予了一个数字。

00:24:47.910 --> 00:25:05.280
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Feroze published an op ed in the New York Times with the results of the survey. A group of the doctors wrote two letters to then President Biden outlining what they saw. Feroze thought that would mean two things-- they'd get a call from the White House and there'd be an investigation.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Feroze在《纽约时报》上发表了一篇专栏文章，公布了调查结果。一组医生写了两封信给当时的拜登总统，概述了他们的所见。Feroze认为这意味着两件事——他们会接到白宫的电话，而且会有调查。

00:25:05.280 --> 00:25:25.740
<v Feroze Sidhwa>I thought that any sensible Biden administration would take that New York Times article as a major opportunity to feign ignorance about what had been going on in Gaza and to say, oh, my God, we can't believe American doctors saw this. And if this is true, then we're going to have to seriously reevaluate the arms that we send to Israel.
<v Feroze Sidhwa>我认为任何明智的拜登政府都会把那篇《纽约时报》文章当作一个重大机会，去假装不知道加沙发生了什么，并说，噢，天哪，我们不敢相信美国医生竟然看到了这一点。如果这是真的，那么我们将不得不重新评估我们送往以色列的武器。

00:25:25.740 --> 00:25:48.480
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>I talked with three people who worked at the US State Department and reviewed allegations like this, including the person who, until recently, was the Ambassador at Large for Global Criminal Justice, a position that used to be called the War Crimes Ambassador. They all agreed the doctors' report sounded credible and significant enough to investigate.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>我采访了三位在美国国务院工作并审查过此类指控的人，包括直到最近还是全球刑事司法无任所大使的人，这个职位过去被称为战争罪行大使。他们都同意医生的报告听起来可信且重要到足以进行调查。

00:25:48.480 --> 00:26:02.790
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Each of them said the next step should be asking Israel for answers. One, who is involved in vetting US weapons transfers, told me if this had been another country other than Israel, this is what would have happened.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>他们每个人都说下一步应该是要求以色列给出答案。其中一位参与审查美国武器转让的人告诉我，如果这发生在除以色列以外的另一个国家，这就是会发生的事情。

00:26:02.790 --> 00:26:26.510
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>A few years ago, a senior member of the House Foreign Affairs Committee received reports that Mexican marine units were executing and disappearing people. The US stopped a shipment of machine guns for more than two years while they investigated the allegations. Eventually, Mexico agreed that American weapons wouldn't go to those units.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>几年前，众议院外交事务委员会的一名高级成员收到报告称，墨西哥海军部队在处决和失踪人员。美国停止了一批机枪的运输超过两年，同时调查这些指控。最终，墨西哥同意美国武器不会流向这些部队。

00:26:26.510 --> 00:26:44.150
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>But that isn't what happened here. The Biden administration never met with Feroze and Mark. They didn't investigate these claims or make any public statements asking Israel to stop shooting kids in Gaza. They didn't try to get to the bottom of these allegations.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>但这在这里并没有发生。拜登政府从未与Feroze和Mark会面。他们没有调查这些主张，也没有发表任何公开声明要求以色列停止在加沙射杀儿童。他们没有试图查清这些指控的真相。

00:26:44.150 --> 00:27:18.890
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>I read these doctors' accounts and had the same question they asked in one of their letters to President Biden. How could this be a series of accidents, such widespread shooting of so many young children over the course of an entire year? But I've also reported on war crimes before, and I know that cases that seem to a bystander, or even a doctor on the ground, like clear acts of atrocity-- they can be explained as a tragic but expected part of war, allowable under the rules of war.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>我读了这些医生的叙述，提出了他们在写给拜登总统的一封信中问的同样的问题：这怎么可能是一系列事故，在整整一年里如此广泛地射杀这么多幼童？但我以前也报道过战争罪行，我知道那些对于旁观者，甚至对于实地医生来说看起来像是清晰的暴行行为的案例——它们可以被解释为战争中悲剧但预料之中的一部分，在战争规则下是允许的。

00:27:18.890 --> 00:27:36.800
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>The Gaza Strip is a small, densely populated place. It's nearly 50% children. Hamas is hiding inside that population. Any fighting between Hamas and Israel will have civilian casualties, including children.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>加沙地带是一个人口稠密的小地方。其中近50%是儿童。哈马斯隐藏在那个人口中。哈马斯和以色列之间的任何战斗都会导致平民伤亡，包括儿童。

00:27:36.800 --> 00:28:11.210
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>So we asked the Israel Defense Forces how they explained the reports from American medical workers. They declined both my interview requests, but sent a statement, saying, "The IDF does not target minors and takes extensive measures to prevent harm to civilians, including children. The IDF is committed to mitigating civilian harm and operates in full compliance with international legal obligations. For security reasons, we cannot elaborate on operational policies."
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>所以我们问以色列国防军他们如何解释美国医务工作者的报告。他们拒绝了我的采访要求，但发了一份声明，称：“以色列国防军不以未成年人为目标，并采取了广泛的措施来防止伤害平民，包括儿童。以色列国防军致力于减轻平民伤害，并完全遵守国际法律义务运营。出于安全原因，我们无法阐述作战政策。”

00:28:11.210 --> 00:28:44.660
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Which doesn't explain why so many kids were getting shot. So I went looking for an Israeli soldier who'd served in Gaza, who could tell me what it was like on the ground and what the rules were on who they could shoot in what circumstances. And we found one who would talk with us. He's a reservist in his early 30s, a grad student whose unit got called up at the beginning of the war. He asked us to just call him M. He was worried about retribution, so we had an actor read his responses to my questions.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>这并没有解释为什么这么多孩子会中弹。所以我去寻找一名曾在加沙服役的以色列士兵，他可以告诉我实地的情况，以及在什么情况下可以射击的规则是什么。我们找到了一个愿意和我们交谈的人。他是一名30岁出头的预备役军人，一名研究生，他的部队在战争初期被征召。他请我们只叫他“M”。他担心受到报复，所以我们请了一位演员朗读他对我问题的回答。

00:28:44.660 --> 00:28:59.720
<v M>It varied a lot, but a typical day was we'd have some missions, looking for weapons, looking for tunnels. And if we see anybody, engage them. And my role was the front machine gunner, light machine gunner.
<v M>情况差异很大，但典型的一天是我们会有一些任务，寻找武器，寻找地道。如果我们看到任何人，就与他们交战。而我的角色是前线机枪手，轻机枪手。

00:28:59.720 --> 00:29:21.450
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>His company started in the north of Gaza, not near the hospital where Feroze and Mark and most of the doctors were. He said there was a humanitarian road running north and south, and near the humanitarian road, the rules of engagement were don't shoot unless there's an immediate threat. But once you got away from the humanitarian road--
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>他的连队始于加沙北部，不在Feroze、Mark和大多数医生所在的医院附近。他说有一条南北向的人道主义道路，在人道主义道路附近，交战规则是除非有直接威胁，否则不要射击。但一旦你远离了人道主义道路——

00:29:21.450 --> 00:29:57.830
<v M>Everything besides that, we're told no one is supposed to be there. The orders to evacuate have been made. Anyone who is there is not supposed to be there. For us, basically the guidance was, men of fighting age, shoot. It doesn't matter if they're armed or not. They're not supposed to be there. And the logic being that Hamas operators, they don't walk around armed. They keep weapons caches in different homes. They walk around. They run to the nearest house if they need a weapon. So any basically men, you shoot on sight.
<v M>除了那之外的一切，我们被告知不应该有任何人。撤离令已经发布。任何在那里的人都不应该在那里。对我们来说，基本上指导原则是，战斗年龄的男性，射击。不管他们是否携带武器。他们不应该在那里。逻辑是，哈马斯人员，他们不会带着武器走动。他们把武器库存放在不同的房子里。他们四处走动。如果需要武器，他们会跑到最近的房子。所以基本上任何男性，你见就射。

00:29:57.830 --> 00:29:58.660
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>And that's--
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>那是——

00:29:58.660 --> 00:30:00.560
<v M>And it's shoot to kill.
<v M>而且是击毙。

00:30:00.560 --> 00:30:01.430
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Shoot to kill.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>击毙。

00:30:01.430 --> 00:30:17.180
<v M>If it's a woman and child, use the standard kind of non-war procedure where you just detain them-- try to detain them. This is how we heard it from the commanders. Don't shoot women and children unless they're a threat. Try to just detain them.
<v M>如果是妇女和儿童，使用标准的非战争程序，你只是拘留他们——试着拘留他们。这是我们从指挥官那里听到的。不要射击妇女和儿童，除非他们是威胁。尽量只是拘留他们。

00:30:17.180 --> 00:30:48.260
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>He says there was one incident where his unit did see two young people they thought might be a threat. One day, when two teenagers, 14 or 15, were part of a convoy going south, they sprinted away and ran into a building. A Hummer and a tank followed them and ordered them to come out. The army thought the teens might be luring them into a trap. When the teens didn't come out, they fired at the building, killing them.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>他说有一次他的部队确实看到了两个他们认为可能是威胁的年轻人。有一天，当两个14或15岁的少年成为向南行进的车队的一部分时，他们冲了出去并跑进了一栋楼。一辆悍马车和一辆坦克跟随他们，命令他们出来。军队认为少年们可能在引诱他们进入陷阱。当少年们没有出来时，他们向建筑物开火，杀死了他们。

00:30:48.260 --> 00:30:57.140
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>He says his unit never fired on any other teenagers or little kids. But, he says, different units operate differently.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>他说他的连队从未向任何其他青少年或小孩开火。但是，他说，不同的部队运作方式不同。

00:30:57.140 --> 00:31:19.190
<v M>The Israeli army is not the US Army. There's much more kind of DIY element. It comes down to your commander, how he decides, the standards he sets. And our company commander happens to be a kibbutznik, who is a little bit more cautious and doesn't want to essentially create a [BLEEP] storm.
<v M>以色列军队不是美国军队。有更多DIY元素。这归结于你的指挥官，他如何决定，他设定的标准。我们的连长恰好是一个基布兹社员，他稍微谨慎一点，不想从本质上制造一场烂摊子。

00:31:19.190 --> 00:31:21.790
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>It matters who your commander is.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>谁是你的指挥官很重要。

00:31:21.790 --> 00:31:32.930
<v M>Yeah. I can tell you there are soldiers in the unit who are hardcore, right wing settlers who, if it was up to them, we could be doing much more damage.
<v M>是的。我可以告诉你，部队里有一些铁杆的、右翼定居者，如果由他们决定，我们可以造成更大的破坏。

00:31:32.930 --> 00:32:02.680
<v M>I remember there was another company in the battalion, their company commander, very much not like ours. I was at an observation post, and he comes by just to look through my binoculars. And he sees a Gazan family passing by some Shaldag soldiers, and he sees them giving the family bottled water. And he goes on the radio to yell at them, saying, what are you doing? You want to open up a lemonade stand, too?
<v M>我记得营里还有另一个连队，他们的连长，非常不像我们那个。我在一个观察哨，他走过来只是为了透过我的望远镜看。他看到一个加沙家庭经过一些Shaldag士兵，他看到他们给那个家庭瓶装水。他在无线电里对他们大喊大叫，说，你们在干什么？你们也想开个柠檬水摊吗？

00:32:02.680 --> 00:32:34.240
<v M>And he starts looking through my binoculars and calling orders to some bulldozers, saying, see that line of trees over there? See that? Take them all down. Destroy all of that. And I ask, oh, are you doing an operation there later and for security, you don't want them to have any kind of sniper positions? And he said, no, just don't let them live. He was pretty much operating from make it as difficult for their lives as possible going forward.
<v M>他开始透过我的望远镜看，并向一些推土机发出命令，说，看到那边那排树了吗？看到那个了吗？把它们全部砍掉。摧毁所有这一切。我问，噢，你们以后要在那里执行行动，为了安全，你们不希望他们有任何狙击阵地吗？他说，不，只是不要让他们活下去。他基本上是在执行“尽可能让他们未来的生活困难”的方针。

00:32:34.240 --> 00:32:58.460
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>So according to M, the broad permission to fire, and the types of units led by more aggressive company commanders, you can see how that could result in soldiers shooting Palestinian children. He didn't see this himself. This is just his opinion, he told me. But he believes that Israeli soldiers have probably shot some children on purpose.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>所以根据M的说法，广泛的射击许可，以及由更具攻击性的连长领导的部队类型，你可以看出这可能导致士兵射杀巴勒斯坦儿童。他自己没有亲眼见到。他告诉我，这只是他的观点。但他相信以色列士兵很可能故意射杀了一些儿童。

00:32:58.460 --> 00:33:15.040
<v M>At least some of these cases I can see being intentional. It wouldn't shock me. They're in the situation, and they get desensitized really fast, and develop a hatred really fast. So no way to justify that. I do understand the mentality.
<v M>至少其中一些案例我认为是有意为之。我不会感到震惊。他们处于这种情况下，他们很快就会脱敏，很快就会产生仇恨。所以无法证明这一点。我确实理解这种心态。

00:33:15.040 --> 00:33:41.800
<v M>At first, shooting unarmed people was horrifying to us. It's like, I can't believe we did that. And then it was like, ugh. Like, you get desensitized to it. And after you lose some friends, you start thinking, why even take the chance? Our lives are more important than theirs. I won't bet my life on it that they're Hamas, but I'll bet their lives on it that they're Hamas, essentially.
<v M>起初，射杀手无寸铁的人让我们感到恐惧。就像是，我不敢相信我们做了那种事。然后就像是，呃。就像，你对它脱敏了。在你失去一些朋友后，你开始想，为什么要冒这个险？我们的生命比他们的重要。我不会拿我的生命打赌他们是哈马斯，但我会拿他们的生命打赌他们是哈马斯，本质上。

00:33:41.800 --> 00:34:02.770
<v M>So regarding this American doctors seeing the children, no, I can only give my take that some soldier took it upon themselves, said, OK, they're an age like this that I can rationalize to myself that they're potentially combatants. Why take the chance?
<v M>所以关于美国医生看到儿童这一点，不，我只能给出我的看法，即某些士兵自作主张，说，好吧，他们处于这种年龄，我可以说服自己他们是潜在的战斗人员。为什么要冒这个险？

00:34:02.770 --> 00:34:15.910
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>I could just tell you, based on the dozen conversations I've had, they're talking about children who are as young as-- this is hard to say-- but 18 months who have been shot.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>我只能告诉你，基于我进行的十几场对话，他们谈论的是像——这很难说——但18个月大的孩子被枪击了。

00:34:15.910 --> 00:34:21.440
<v M>Well, the 18-month-old, there's no-- there's no question there.
<v M>好吧，18个月大的那个，没有任何——没有任何疑问。

00:34:21.440 --> 00:34:22.280
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Yeah.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>是的。

00:34:22.280 --> 00:34:48.190
<v M>And I don't think anything-- I can't give a reason. I mean, the simple one of crossfire, et cetera, that's one. But if they're saying there was one shot exactly and no other wounds, then that goes out the window.
<v M>而且我不认为任何——我无法给出理由。我的意思是，简单的交叉火力之类的，这是一种。但如果他们说是一次射击且没有其他伤口，那么这就站不住脚了。

00:34:48.190 --> 00:35:00.070
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>I asked M for other possible explanations. What if the soldiers didn't know they were kids? It's a chaotic, tense environment full of people and places to hide. That could happen.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>我问M有没有其他可能的解释。如果士兵不知道他们是孩子呢？这是一个混乱、紧张的环境，到处都是人和可以躲藏的地方。那可能会发生。

00:35:00.070 --> 00:35:23.880
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>But he and another IDF soldier I spoke with, someone who didn't serve in Gaza but whose job was to identify targets for snipers, said it wasn't hard to distinguish a child from an adult. It wouldn't explain an 18-month-old being precisely shot in the head, or toddlers, or other grade school age kids.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>但他和另一位我采访过的以色列国防军士兵，一个没在加沙服役但工作是为狙击手识别目标的人，都说区分孩子和成年人并不难。这无法解释一个18个月大的孩子被精确地射击头部，或者幼儿，或其他学龄儿童。

00:35:23.880 --> 00:35:47.370
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Were the kids being used as human shields by Hamas, or even victims of Hamas in a false flag operation to blame Israel and draw sympathy? M hadn't heard of that happening in Gaza and hadn't seen it. He says when he tries to make sense of what happened, there's been a societal shift in Israel that he thinks is a factor.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>这些孩子是被哈马斯用作人盾，甚至是哈马斯在假旗行动中的受害者，用以指责以色列并博取同情吗？M没听说过加沙发生过这种事，也没见过。他说当他试图理解发生的事情时，以色列社会发生了转变，他认为这是一个因素。

00:35:47.370 --> 00:35:57.630
<v M>I hear enough attitudes and opinions that would have shocked me 10 years ago. Now, it's like yeah, this is how people feel here.
<v M>我听到了足够的态度和观点，这在10年前会让我震惊。现在，就像是是的，这就是这里人们的感觉。

00:35:57.630 --> 00:36:29.100
<v M>I have a friend whose dad said in an interview he was in the Mossad and basically said that no one in Gaza over the age of 4 is innocent. So this isn't a fringe mentality among Israelis. No one in Gaza is innocent. They all support Hamas, of all ages. Where they draw the line varies. But they'll say anyone that's self-aware in Gaza is a Hamas supporter.
<v M>我有一个朋友，他的父亲在一次采访中说他在摩萨德工作，基本上说在加沙，4岁以上的人没有一个是无辜的。所以这在以色列人中不是一种边缘心态。在加沙，没有人是无辜的。他们都支持哈马斯，无论年龄大小。他们划定界限的地方各不相同。但他们会说在加沙任何有自我意识的人都是哈马斯支持者。

00:36:29.100 --> 00:36:52.920
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>The Israeli army has not announced any investigations into the shooting of children in Gaza. In the meantime, the Trump administration just authorized another $12 billion in arms sales to Israel. That's in addition to the reported $17.9 billion the US has given Israel in military aid since the war began.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>以色列军队尚未宣布对加沙射杀儿童的事件进行任何调查。与此同时，特朗普政府刚刚批准了向以色列出售价值120亿美元的武器。这还不包括自战争开始以来美国向以色列提供的据报道价值179亿美元的军事援助。

00:36:52.920 --> 00:37:12.780
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>The kids getting shot like this are a tiny part of the large number of casualties in Gaza. At least 65,000 people have been killed, more than 15,000 of them children. An expert who investigates war crimes described it as a haystack of needles.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>像这样中枪的孩子只是加沙大量伤亡中的一小部分。至少有65,000人被杀，其中超过15,000人是儿童。一位调查战争罪行的专家将其描述为“草堆里的针”。

00:37:12.780 --> 00:37:36.720
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Most of these allegations will never find their way to court, so sometimes, in cases like these, war crimes investigators look for emblematic crimes, clear instances of violence outside of the rules of war that tell us something about the war, like the My Lai Massacre, Srebrenica, the chemical weapons attacks in Syria.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>这些指控中的大多数永远不会进入法庭，所以有时，在这些案例中，战争罪行调查人员会寻找具有象征意义的罪行，即战争规则之外的明确暴力实例，这些实例告诉我们一些关于这场战争的信息，比如美莱村大屠杀、斯雷布雷尼察、叙利亚的化学武器袭击。

00:37:36.720 --> 00:38:11.350
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>What do these deaths tell us? The hundreds of kids American medical workers saw shot in the head and chest, what are they emblematic of? They tell us it's likely that an Israeli soldier can shoot a child in Gaza on purpose with no consequence, and the US isn't going to try to stop it.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>这些死亡告诉我们什么？数百名美国医务工作者看到头部和胸部中枪的孩子，它们象征着什么？它们告诉我们，以色列士兵很可能在加沙故意射杀一名儿童而不受任何惩罚，而美国并不打算阻止它。

00:38:11.350 --> 00:38:18.370
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Ike Sriskandarajah is a producer on our show. Avi Vichner is the actor who read for M.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Ike Sriskandarajah是我们节目的制作人。Avi Vichner是为M朗读的演员。

00:38:18.370 --> 00:38:33.130
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Coming up, a woman discovers she is a central data point in a story she did not know was unfolding. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>接下来，一名女子发现自己是她并不知道正在展开的故事中的一个核心数据点。一分钟后，来自芝加哥公共广播电台，当我们的节目继续时。

00:38:33.130 --> 00:38:50.490
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It's This American Life. I'm Chana Joffe-Walt sitting in for Ira Glass. Today's show, Chaos Graph, people trying to make sense of things, trying to map what is happening around them and see the bigger picture. We've arrived at Act Two-- Solve for Where.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这里是《美国生活》。我是Chana Joffe-Walt，代替Ira Glass。今天的节目，“混乱图表”，人们试图理清事情，试图绘制他们周围正在发生的事情并看到更大的图景。我们到达了第二幕——求解“在哪里”。

00:38:50.490 --> 00:39:17.640
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>In this next story, the bigger picture has become very clear to the rest of us over the last couple of weeks. It's in the news all the time. But the woman at the center of this story, she experienced it up close. She learned each fact in the most personal, direct way, and making sense of those facts, understanding how they all fit together for her was urgent. Nadia Reiman has her story.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>在这个接下来的故事中，更大的图景在过去几周里对我们其他人来说变得非常清晰。新闻里到处都是。但这个故事中心的那位女性，她近距离经历了这一切。她以最个人、最直接的方式学习了每一个事实，而理解这些事实，理解它们如何为她拼凑在一起是紧迫的。Nadia Reiman带来了她的故事。

00:39:17.640 --> 00:39:40.370
<v Nadia Reiman>When Mari shows me this video, it feels like something I'm not supposed to be watching. On the screen is her fiance, Mikael. He's shirtless. He's crisply lined up. He's young, 23, bright green eyes, wild curly hair tucked under a baseball cap, and he's holding a tattoo gun, giving himself a tattoo on his stomach while she teases him, says he's never going to tattoo her again.
<v Nadia Reiman>当Mari给我看这段视频时，感觉就像我不应该看的东西。屏幕上是她的未婚夫，Mikael。他赤裸着上身。他身材匀称。他很年轻，23岁，有着明亮的绿色眼睛，蓬乱的卷发塞在棒球帽下，他手里拿着纹身枪，正在给自己的肚子纹身，而她逗他，说他再也不会纹她了。

00:39:40.370 --> 00:39:43.380
<v Nadia Reiman>[MIKAEL LAUGHING]
<v Nadia Reiman>[MIKAEL大笑]

00:39:43.380 --> 00:39:55.680
<v Nadia Reiman>He looks up and laughs, his mouth full of braces. Mari is always sort of dragging Mikael about his tattoos. He has lots of them, some which Mari finds offensively dumb.
<v Nadia Reiman>他抬头大笑，满嘴牙套。Mari总是因为Mikael的纹身而对他有点“拖后腿”。他有很多纹身，Mari觉得有些令人反感地愚蠢。

00:39:55.680 --> 00:39:58.700
<v Mari>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Mari>[说西班牙语]

00:39:58.700 --> 00:40:07.710
<v Interpreter>He has a Mickey Mouse, but the Mickey Mouse is smoking. He used to smoke, and he used to do ridiculous things like that, because to me, they're ridiculous things. But yeah.
<v Interpreter>他有一个米老鼠，但米老鼠在抽烟。他以前抽烟，而且他过去做一些类似那样荒谬的事情，因为对我来说，它们是荒谬的事情。但是，是的。

00:40:07.710 --> 00:40:17.300
<v Interpreter>And then behind that, he has a little hand, like a little hand with an eye. And he tells me that eye sees everything. And I was just like, bueno, OK.
<v Interpreter>在那之后，他有一只小手，像一只带着眼睛的小手。他告诉我那只眼睛能看见一切。我就说，好的，好吧。

00:40:17.300 --> 00:40:20.460
<v Mari>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Mari>[说西班牙语]

00:40:20.460 --> 00:40:28.260
<v Interpreter>And he has something else there, like just-- like an ugly doll, like it looks like a toad. It has big eyes.
<v Interpreter>他那里还有别的东西，像……就像一个丑娃娃，看起来像一只蟾蜍。它有大眼睛。

00:40:28.260 --> 00:40:30.110
<v Mari>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Mari>[说西班牙语]

00:40:30.110 --> 00:40:44.840
<v Nadia Reiman>It's ET smoking a blunt. Mari is not her real name. She's from Venezuela. She's here applying for asylum, which is legal, but that seems to matter less and less nowadays in terms of who gets deported, hence the alias.
<v Nadia Reiman>那是外星人在抽大麻。Mari不是她的真名。她来自委内瑞拉。她在这里申请庇护，这是合法的，但考虑到谁会被驱逐出境，这似乎在当今变得越来越不重要，因此用了化名。

00:40:44.840 --> 00:41:13.370
<v Nadia Reiman>Mari and Mikael are the kind couple who wear matching outfits-- her idea, but she insists he's also into it, shows me a picture where they're wearing matching pink shorts that he chose. Mikael is a barber. Mari works cleaning hotels, so they work opposite hours. She gets up early, goes to bed early. He gets up later, goes to bed later. When their schedules do match up, they're usually so tired that they sit at home and watch TV on their fuzzy pink bedspread.
<v Nadia Reiman>Mari和Mikael是那种穿情侣装的可爱夫妻——她的主意，但她坚持说他也喜欢，给我看了一张照片，照片里他们穿着他挑的粉色情侣短裤。Mikael是理发师。Mari在酒店做清洁工作，所以他们的工作时间是错开的。她起得早，睡得早。他起得晚，睡得晚。当他们的时间表一致时，他们通常累得坐在一起在他们毛茸茸的粉色床上看电视。

00:41:13.370 --> 00:41:25.740
<v Nadia Reiman>The morning it happened, Mari left early, like she usually does. She noticed a bunch of men outside. They were dressed in black, she says. One of them spoke Spanish. They came up to her, her roommate, and her landlord.
<v Nadia Reiman>事情发生的那天早上，Mari像往常一样早早离开了。她注意到外面有一群男人。她说他们穿着黑色衣服。其中一个说西班牙语。他们走到她、她的室友和她的房东面前。

00:41:25.740 --> 00:41:27.820
<v Mari>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Mari>[说西班牙语]

00:41:27.820 --> 00:41:42.200
<v Interpreter>The owner of the house stands up, and he looks out the window, and he sees a few police outside. And they show him a photo, and they ask him for un muchacho, for a guy. And he says, no, he doesn't live here, but three other people live here.
<v Interpreter>房主站起来，向窗外看，他看到外面有几个警察。他们给他看了一张照片，问他要一个年轻人。他说，不，他不住在这里，但这里住了其他三个人。

00:41:42.200 --> 00:41:44.450
<v Mari>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Mari>[说西班牙语]

00:41:44.450 --> 00:41:51.830
<v Nadia Reiman>It seemed like they were looking for someone. One of them showed her the photo on his phone. He was like, do you know this person?
<v Nadia Reiman>看起来他们在找人。其中一个在手机上给她看了照片。他说，你认识这个人吗？

00:41:51.830 --> 00:41:54.810
<v Mari>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Mari>[说西班牙语]

00:41:54.810 --> 00:42:03.600
<v Interpreter>He was a dark-skinned person with dark hair. He had big eyes. He had a beard. We didn't really know who he was. And they tell us that he's Dominican.
<v Interpreter>他是一个皮肤黝黑、头发深色的人。他有大眼睛。他留着胡子。我们真的不知道他是谁。他们告诉我们他是多米尼加人。

00:42:03.600 --> 00:42:04.850
<v Mari>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Mari>[说西班牙语]

00:42:04.850 --> 00:42:18.510
<v Interpreter>And he tells us that they're going to check the house to just make sure and to verify, to make sure it's true that that person that they're looking for doesn't live here. I really didn't see a problem with it because we haven't committed any crime. We're not hiding anything.
<v Interpreter>他告诉我们他们要搜查房子，只是为了确认并核实，确保他们正在找的那个人真的不住在这里。我真的没觉得有什么问题，因为我们没有犯任何罪。我们没有隐瞒任何事情。

00:42:18.510 --> 00:42:20.930
<v Mari>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Mari>[说西班牙语]

00:42:20.930 --> 00:42:42.650
<v Nadia Reiman>Mari figured she and Mikael had applied for asylum. He had his first court hearing already. His next one was coming up. They didn't think they were in any danger, so Mari sees no problem with letting any sort of law enforcement in. So she opens the gate, leads them down these narrow gray steps and into her basement apartment, where Mikael was still asleep.
<v Nadia Reiman>Mari想，她和Mikael已经申请了庇护。他已经完成了第一次法庭听证。他的下一次听证会即将到来。他们不觉得自己在危险之中，所以Mari觉得让任何类型的执法人员进来都没有问题。所以她打开大门，带他们走下狭窄的灰色台阶，进入她的地下室公寓，Mikael还在那里睡觉。

00:42:42.650 --> 00:42:45.240
<v Mari>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Mari>[说西班牙语]

00:42:45.240 --> 00:43:06.200
<v Interpreter>So they went downstairs, and, obviously, I went first, because I was there to wake him up. He was half asleep, and he says, what's happening? He sat up, and when he sat up, well, he usually sleeps naked. I hand him a towel, and I tell him put the towel on because [SPEAKING SPANISH]. The police is coming.
<v Interpreter>他们下楼了，显然我走在前面，因为我是去叫醒他的。他睡眼朦胧，他说，怎么了？他坐起来，当他坐起来时，好吧，他通常裸睡。我递给他一条毛巾，告诉他把毛巾围上，因为……[说西班牙语]。警察来了。

00:43:06.200 --> 00:43:07.940
<v Mari>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Mari>[说西班牙语]

00:43:07.940 --> 00:43:18.180
<v Interpreter>They came in. They pushed the door. They put me in handcuffs. They put him in handcuffs. And the moment that happened, I said, well, what's happening? Why are you putting us in handcuffs?
<v Interpreter>他们进来了。他们推开了门。他们给我戴上手铐。他们给他戴上手铐。那一刻，我说，好吧，发生了什么？你们为什么要给我们戴上手铐？

00:43:18.180 --> 00:43:28.950
<v Interpreter>And then I started crying. I started crying, and he would tell me, niña, tranquila. Niña, calm down. And I kept on crying, and I was asking, why are we in handcuffs? Why are they doing this? But they didn't tell me anything.
<v Interpreter>然后我开始哭。我开始哭，他会告诉我，小姑娘，冷静点。小姑娘，冷静下来。我一直在哭，我在问，为什么我们戴着手铐？为什么他们要这样做？但他们什么都没告诉我。

00:43:28.950 --> 00:43:40.100
<v Interpreter>The only thing they were saying, stay calm, because things can get worse. And if he moved, whatever movement he made, they would sit him down. I mean, he couldn't do anything. He couldn't move.
<v Interpreter>他们唯一说的是，保持冷静，因为事情可能会变得更糟。如果他移动，无论他做了什么动作，他们就会让他坐下。我的意思是，他什么也做不了。他无法移动。

00:43:40.100 --> 00:43:43.770
<v Nadia Reiman>Did they identify themselves? Did they tell you they were from ICE?
<v Nadia Reiman>他们表明身份了吗？他们告诉你他们是移民局的吗？

00:43:43.770 --> 00:43:51.350
<v Mari>No.
<v Mari>没有。

00:43:51.350 --> 00:44:04.580
<v Nadia Reiman>The men in black, Mari would later learn, were maybe ICE, Immigrations and Customs Enforcement, or maybe police working with ICE and the DEA for some reason. We don't know for sure, because they won't answer any of my questions.
<v Nadia Reiman>Mari后来才知道，那些黑衣人可能是移民和海关执法局（ICE），或者是出于某种原因与ICE和缉毒局（DEA）合作的警察。我们不能确定，因为他们不会回答我的任何问题。

00:44:04.580 --> 00:44:20.870
<v Nadia Reiman>What we do know is that Mikael ends up one of the 238 men who are in the news now almost every day, men who were sent away to a prison in El Salvador, where no one can reach them. And the president has refused to try to get them back, despite a court suggesting he should.
<v Nadia Reiman>我们所知道的是，Mikael最终成为了现在每天新闻里都会提到的238名男子之一，这些男子被送往萨尔瓦多的一所监狱，在那里没有人能联系到他们。尽管有法院建议应该努力接回他们，但总统拒绝了。

00:44:20.870 --> 00:44:32.190
<v Nadia Reiman>But that's all in the future. On that day in February, Mari says there was one of them, one of the men in black, that spoke Spanish. He started talking to Mikael. He was calmer.
<v Nadia Reiman>但那都是以后的事了。在二月的那一天，Mari说他们中有一个人，那个说西班牙语的黑衣人，开始和Mikael交谈。他更冷静。

00:44:32.190 --> 00:44:49.550
<v Nadia Reiman>He was like, it's OK, man, just be cool. Don't move too much. It's going to be fine. He translates for the rest. He tells Mari that they are taking Mikael, that they need to question him, but that she is free to go. Mari is in a daze. She doesn't know why they are taking him but letting her go.
<v Nadia Reiman>他说，没关系，伙计，酷一点。不要动太多。会没事的。他为其他人翻译。他告诉Mari，他们要带走Mikael，他们需要盘问他，但她可以自由离开。Mari处于恍惚状态。她不知道他们为什么带走他却放她走。

00:44:49.550 --> 00:45:07.660
<v Nadia Reiman>They uncuff her. They let Mikael get dressed. The one that speaks Spanish tells Mari that they're taking him to Federal Plaza, an ICE office in Manhattan. And then, it's over. The men in uniforms take Mikael away, and Mari is left looking around, wondering what just happened.
<v Nadia Reiman>他们解开她的手铐。他们让Mikael穿好衣服。那个说西班牙语的人告诉Mari，他们要把他带到联邦广场，那是位于曼哈顿的ICE办公室。然后，一切都结束了。身着制服的男人们带走了Mikael，Mari留在原地环顾四周，想知道刚才发生了什么。

00:45:07.660 --> 00:45:09.040
<v Mari>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Mari>[说西班牙语]

00:45:09.040 --> 00:45:14.000
<v Interpreter>I didn't know what to do, so the first thing I did was-- I don't know. I went to work.
<v Interpreter>我不知道该怎么办，所以我做的第一件事是……我不知道。我去工作了。

00:45:14.000 --> 00:45:15.730
<v Nadia Reiman>You went to work after that?
<v Nadia Reiman>在那之后你去工作了？

00:45:15.730 --> 00:45:17.180
<v Mari>Si. Si.
<v Mari>是的。是的。

00:45:17.180 --> 00:45:19.180
<v Nadia Reiman>Why?
<v Nadia Reiman>为什么？

00:45:19.180 --> 00:45:20.210
<v Mari>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Mari>[说西班牙语]

00:45:20.210 --> 00:45:33.550
<v Interpreter>I didn't know what to do. I thought to myself, look, I'll go to work. I'll look for help. I just didn't know what to do. I didn't know. Everything felt stuck. I've never gone through something like this.
<v Interpreter>我不知道该怎么办。我对自己说，看，我去工作。我会寻求帮助。我只是不知道该怎么办。我不知道。一切都感觉被卡住了。我从来没有经历过这样的事情。

00:45:33.550 --> 00:45:51.700
<v Nadia Reiman>Not going to work is not an option for Mari. She's been working since she was about 16, no matter what's happening around her. Her boss told me that she's never met someone so young yet so serious, reliable. She's the only one in her family who has left the country, the problem solver, the one her mom likes best, she said.
<v Nadia Reiman>不去工作对Mari来说不是一个选择。无论周围发生了什么，她从16岁左右就开始工作了。她的老板告诉我，她从未见过如此年轻却又如此认真、可靠的人。她是她家里唯一离开国家的人，是解决问题的人，是她妈妈最喜欢的人，她说。

00:45:51.700 --> 00:45:59.110
<v Nadia Reiman>I asked her, would your mom agree? And without missing a beat, she said, I will FaceTime her right now and you can ask her yourself.
<v Nadia Reiman>我问她，你妈妈会同意吗？她毫不犹豫地说，我现在就跟她视频通话，你可以亲自问她。

00:45:59.110 --> 00:46:12.280
<v Nadia Reiman>Mari met Mikael in Colombia three years ago. She was 21. He kept hanging out at the boutique where she worked and one day, she mentioned she wanted a tattoo. He was like, I'll do it for you. She was skeptical but agreed.
<v Nadia Reiman>Mari三年前在哥伦比亚遇到了Mikael。她当时21岁。他总是去她工作的精品店，有一天，她提到想要纹身。他说，我帮你纹。她持怀疑态度，但还是同意了。

00:46:12.280 --> 00:46:23.530
<v Nadia Reiman>From the moment they started dating, they were inseparable. Mari told me she has a temper and liked that Mikael could handle it. She says he's funny and charming, a little more shameless than she is.
<v Nadia Reiman>从他们开始约会的那一刻起，他们就形影不离。Mari告诉我她脾气暴躁，喜欢Mikael能应付她的脾气。她说他既风趣又有魅力，比她稍微厚脸皮一点。

00:46:23.530 --> 00:46:32.110
<v Nadia Reiman>When they traveled up to the US, if they needed to ask for food, Mari always felt embarrassed. Not Mikael. He made friends with everybody.
<v Nadia Reiman>当他们前往美国时，如果他们需要要食物，Mari总是觉得尴尬。Mikael不会。他与每个人都成了朋友。

00:46:32.110 --> 00:47:02.800
<v Nadia Reiman>When they got to New York and their hours didn't match up, they spent what seems to me like basically their whole day video calling. When she got off the train in the morning, call from Mikael. When he got out of the shower, call from Mikael. At lunchtime, call from Mikael. While he's cutting hair, call from Mikael. They knew where the other was, what the other was doing, what the other was eating all the time, until now.
<v Nadia Reiman>当他们到达纽约，工作时间错开时，他们花的时间看起来就像几乎整天都在视频通话。早上她下火车时，有Mikael的电话。他洗完澡出来时，有Mikael的电话。午饭时间，有Mikael的电话。当他在剪头发时，有Mikael的电话。他们一直知道对方在哪里，在做什么，在吃什么，直到现在。

00:47:02.800 --> 00:47:20.980
<v Nadia Reiman>Mari is at a loss. She doesn't understand why Mikael would be in trouble. So she turns to the first person she trusts that knows this crazy system to see if she can help her make sense of what's going on, her boss at the hotel, a woman from Ecuador who's also an immigrant but a citizen.
<v Nadia Reiman>Mari束手无策。她不明白Mikael为什么会有麻烦。所以她求助于她信任的第一个了解这种疯狂系统的人，看看她能不能帮她弄清楚发生了什么，就是她在酒店的老板，一位来自厄瓜多尔的女性，她也是移民，但已经是公民了。

00:47:20.980 --> 00:47:32.140
<v Nadia Reiman>Her boss always told her employees if they were ever in trouble with immigration, come to her, no problem. So even though it made Mari feel a little embarrassed to ask for help, she did it.
<v Nadia Reiman>她的老板总是告诉员工，如果他们遇到移民问题，就来找她，没问题。所以尽管这让Mari觉得请求帮助有点尴尬，但她还是做了。

00:47:32.140 --> 00:47:46.330
<v Nadia Reiman>Mari's boss helped Mari find Mikael online through an ICE website. And it shows that, indeed, Mikael is being held in New York. That afternoon, he calls and tells Mari that they had to hold him there, even though there was no criminal record of him.
<v Nadia Reiman>Mari的老板帮Mari在网上通过ICE网站找到了Mikael。它确实显示Mikael被关在纽约。那天下午，他打电话告诉Mari，尽管他没有犯罪记录，但他们必须把他关在那里。

00:47:46.330 --> 00:47:54.010
<v Nadia Reiman>That night, Mari tracks him online again and sees that he's now in Pennsylvania. She thinks, why are they moving him?
<v Nadia Reiman>那天晚上，Mari再次在网上追踪他，看到他现在在宾夕法尼亚州。她想，为什么要转移他？

00:47:54.010 --> 00:48:11.920
<v Nadia Reiman>Mari's boss tells her get a lawyer, like yesterday, so she starts to look for one. She needs one that she can afford and that speaks Spanish. It took her a week to find one. The lawyer looks into Mikael's case right away, trying to figure out why he got detained in the first place.
<v Nadia Reiman>Mari的老板告诉她赶紧找个律师，于是她开始找。她需要一个她负担得起且讲西班牙语的律师。她花了一周时间才找到。律师立即开始研究Mikael的案件，试图弄清楚他当初为什么被拘留。

00:48:11.920 --> 00:48:28.610
<v Nadia Reiman>Much to the lawyer's distaste, Mari, a true gen Z'er, starts to post a lot on TikTok. Almost overnight, her feed morphs from dancing reggaeton videos to photo collages of Mikael set to cheesy Spanish ballads, laying out their story, asking people to make it go viral.
<v Nadia Reiman>令律师不悦的是，作为一个真正的Z世代，Mari开始在TikTok上大量发帖。几乎一夜之间，她的动态就从雷鬼舞视频变成了配有俗气西班牙语情歌的Mikael照片拼贴，展示了他们的故事，要求人们让它疯传。

00:48:28.610 --> 00:48:35.340
<v Mari>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Mari>[说西班牙语]

00:48:35.340 --> 00:48:52.870
<v Nadia Reiman>Mari is particularly concerned with Mikael being moved to Pennsylvania like that, without him consenting to it. The lawyer tells her, don't worry about that part. The government can move him around detention centers all they want. They just can't deport him. A judge has to decide that.
<v Nadia Reiman>Mari特别担心Mikael被转移到宾夕法尼亚州，他并没有同意。律师告诉她，不要担心那部分。政府可以随意把他转移到各个拘留中心。他们只是不能驱逐他。法官必须决定。

00:48:52.870 --> 00:49:02.620
<v Nadia Reiman>So for the next few weeks, all of these moving parts become Mari's new normal-- TikToks about Mikael, calls with the lawyer, video calls from the Pennsylvania detention center.
<v Nadia Reiman>所以接下来的几周，所有这些变动的组成部分成了Mari的新常态——关于Mikael的TikTok，与律师的通话，来自宾夕法尼亚拘留中心的视频电话。

00:49:02.620 --> 00:49:06.400
<v Mari>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Mari>[说西班牙语]

00:49:06.400 --> 00:49:13.670
<v Interpreter>Four or five times a day was him calling me, me calming him down, me telling him he was going to get out of there, to have faith.
<v Interpreter>一天四五次是他打电话给我，我安慰他，我告诉他他会出来的，要相信。

00:49:13.670 --> 00:49:16.890
<v Mari>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Mari>[说西班牙语]

00:49:16.890 --> 00:49:21.980
<v Nadia Reiman>And then, one day in March, she doesn't hear from him for hours.
<v Nadia Reiman>然后，三月的一天，她几个小时没收到他的消息。

00:49:21.980 --> 00:49:25.370
<v Mari>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Mari>[说西班牙语]

00:49:25.370 --> 00:49:48.210
<v Interpreter>And then he called me. He called me at 4:00 in the morning, and he tells me, crying desperately, niña, they transferred me again to El Valle, to Texas. I was the whole time on a plane. They didn't tell me anything. I didn't eat. I didn't drink water. They didn't give us anything. I haven't been able to sleep. They're going to process me here.
<v Interpreter>然后他打电话给我。他在凌晨4点打电话给我，绝望地哭着告诉我，小姑娘，他们又把我转移到El Valle了，去德克萨斯州。我全程都在飞机上。他们什么都没告诉我。我没吃东西。我没喝水。他们什么都没给我们。我一直没法睡觉。他们要在这里处理我。

00:49:48.210 --> 00:50:02.460
<v Interpreter>And me, bueno niño, calm down. Be strong. You're going to leave. Remember that on Monday, you have your court appointment. If we have to pay bail, we'll pay bail. But you're going to-- you're going to leave. You're going to get out of there.
<v Interpreter>而我，好的小姑娘，冷静点。要坚强。你会离开的。记住周一你有法庭预约。如果我们必须付保释金，我们会付保释金。但你会——你会离开的。你会从那里出来的。

00:50:02.460 --> 00:50:13.980
<v Nadia Reiman>Mari checks the website, and indeed, there he is in Texas, so much further away from her, from their little basement apartment. She's like, why are they taking him further away?
<v Nadia Reiman>Mari查了网站，确实，他在德克萨斯州，离她、离他们的小地下室公寓远得多了。她说，为什么要带他去更远的地方？

00:50:13.980 --> 00:50:29.040
<v Nadia Reiman>She calls the lawyer, who tells her, again, don't worry, they can move him, but they can't deport him. Which is true, at least legally speaking. Mari had a bad feeling, but she pushed it down and went to work.
<v Nadia Reiman>她打电话给律师，律师又告诉她，不要担心，他们可以转移他，但他们不能驱逐他。这是事实，至少在法律层面上是这样。Mari有一种不好的预感，但她把它压了下去，去工作了。

00:50:29.040 --> 00:50:35.230
<v Mari>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Mari>[说西班牙语]

00:50:35.230 --> 00:50:56.500
<v Interpreter>I worked as much as I could, because honestly, when you're nervous, when you're anxious, it's really, really hard to get things done. I arrived really late to my house that day. I arrived at 8 o'clock. I arrived on the bus. When I get down off the bus on the corner of my house, my boss, she was the one that sent me the video.
<v Interpreter>我尽我所能工作，因为说实话，当你紧张，当你焦虑时，做事真的非常非常难。那天我回家很晚。我8点到的。我乘公共汽车。当我下公共汽车走到家门口时，我的老板，是她发给我的视频。

00:50:56.500 --> 00:51:04.660
<v Interpreter>She tells me, Mari, I sent you this video. I think I saw your husband there. Take a look and let me know if it's him.
<v Interpreter>她告诉我，Mari，我发给你这个视频。我想我看到你丈夫在那里了。看看并让我知道是不是他。

00:51:04.660 --> 00:51:07.500
<v Nadia Reiman>She sent it to your phone, like a video on your phone?
<v Nadia Reiman>她把它发到你手机上了，就像手机里的视频吗？

00:51:07.500 --> 00:51:08.340
<v Mari>Si.
<v Mari>是的。

00:51:08.340 --> 00:51:31.670
<v Interpreter>Yeah, it was a news clip, from Telemundo. And I click on the video, and the beginning is there were airplanes, getting to El Salvador, and a bunch of Venezuelans, people getting off the planes and all of that. But at the end, when the video was almost done, there's a moment where there's people kneeling down, getting shaved.
<v Interpreter>是的，那是一个来自Telemundo的新闻片段。我点击视频，开头是飞机，到达萨尔瓦多，一堆委内瑞拉人，人们下飞机等等。但在最后，当视频快结束时，有一个人们跪下被剃头的时刻。

00:51:31.670 --> 00:51:36.060
<v Mari>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Mari>[说西班牙语]

00:51:36.060 --> 00:51:55.330
<v Nadia Reiman>There, at 38 seconds in on the screen, was Mikael, profile sharp, curls falling around his face, his head held down while someone shaves his head. It lasts just a few seconds, and then the camera cuts to someone else. It was chilling, like a scene in some perverse movie.
<v Nadia Reiman>那里，屏幕上38秒处，是Mikael，侧脸轮廓分明，卷发垂在脸颊周围，低着头，有人在给他剃头。它只持续了几秒钟，然后镜头切到了别人。这令人不寒而栗，就像某种反常电影里的场景。

00:51:55.330 --> 00:51:56.880
<v Mari>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Mari>[说西班牙语]

00:51:56.880 --> 00:52:18.370
<v Interpreter>I recognized him immediately. The second I saw him, I knew it was him. He has a tattoo, and it's a Chinese phrase, like on his sideburn. And, obviously, when they were shaving him, you could see it. And he has long, curly blonde hair, and he's the only one who had hair like that. I mean, it was him. It was him.
<v Interpreter>我立刻认出了他。我看到他的那一秒，我就知道是他。他有一个纹身，是中文短语，就在他的鬓角上。显然，当他们剃头时，你能看到它。他有着长长的、卷曲的金发，他是唯一一个有那样头发的人。我的意思是，是他。是他。

00:52:18.370 --> 00:52:27.280
<v Mari>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Mari>[说西班牙语]

00:52:27.280 --> 00:52:48.000
<v Interpreter>When I saw him, I went into crisis. I started shaking. I started crying. I was with another girl that lives with me, because we work in the same hotel. And I tell her, Josefina, it's him. Josefina, it's him. It's Mikael. They took him, and I just-- it was very bad.
<v Interpreter>当我看到他时，我陷入了危机。我开始发抖。我开始哭。我和住在一起的另一个女孩在一起，因为我们在同一家酒店工作。我告诉她，Josefina，是他。Josefina，是他。是Mikael。他们带走了他，我只是……情况非常糟糕。

00:52:48.000 --> 00:52:50.880
<v Mari>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Mari>[说西班牙语]

00:52:50.880 --> 00:53:02.290
<v Interpreter>I was telling her, Jose, it's him, it's him, it's him. And the first thing she told me was calm down, because something could happen to you. And all I could say was, Jose, it's him. They took him.
<v Interpreter>我告诉她，Jose，是他，是他，是他。她对我说的第一件事就是冷静点，因为你可能会出事。而我只能说，Jose，是他。他们带走了他。

00:53:02.290 --> 00:53:11.350
<v Interpreter>I just felt so, so sad when I saw his face, like he was suffering. His eyes were swollen. He must have cried so much.
<v Interpreter>看到他的脸，我感到如此、如此悲伤，就像他在受苦一样。他的眼睛肿了。他一定哭得很厉害。

00:53:11.350 --> 00:53:20.900
<v Interpreter>And then immediately, I went home. I turned on my computer, and I started to look, and immediately, I saw that he was-- he was no longer in the US system.
<v Interpreter>然后立刻，我回家了。我打开电脑，开始看，我立刻看到他——他不再在美国系统里了。

00:53:20.900 --> 00:53:32.700
<v Nadia Reiman>At that moment, Mari unknowingly became a data point, part of a group of people who figured out their loved ones were taken to a notoriously bad Salvadoran prison through a video.
<v Nadia Reiman>那一刻，Mari不知不觉地成为了一个数据点，成为了一群通过视频发现亲人被带到臭名昭著的萨尔瓦多监狱的人中的一员。

00:53:32.700 --> 00:53:46.380
<v Nadia Reiman>A mom in Venezuela had the same experience at around the same time as Mari. She saw a close-up of her son Mervin Yamarte Fernandez's face on screen in the same Telemundo report, and that's how she knew where he was.
<v Nadia Reiman>委内瑞拉的一位母亲在大约与Mari相同的时间经历了同样的经历。她在同一个Telemundo报道中看到了屏幕上她儿子Mervin Yamarte Fernandez脸部的特写，那就是她知道他身在何处的方式。

00:53:46.380 --> 00:54:09.180
<v Nadia Reiman>Another mom, same thing. Seeing a brief glimpse of his tattooed arms was the only marker of her son Francisco Garcia Casique and his whereabouts. Without these videos, they would have simply disappeared.
<v Nadia Reiman>另一位母亲，同样的事情。瞥见他纹身的胳膊是她儿子Francisco Garcia Casique及其下落的唯一标记。没有这些视频，他们就会简单地消失。

00:54:09.180 --> 00:54:41.780
<v Nadia Reiman>I know people say unprecedented a lot, but I really can't think of another word to describe this swift and secretive removal of 238 Venezuelans. It happened so quickly. Within four hours of the Trump administration invoking the Alien Enemies Act, Mikael and the other 237 got removed. Mari described it to me as a kidnapping. And this move of shipping people who were in the US abroad to some dank prison is unlike anything I've seen in US immigration.
<v Nadia Reiman>我知道人们常说“前所未有”，但我真的想不出另一个词来描述这种对238名委内瑞拉人迅速而秘密的移送。发生得太快了。特朗普政府援引《外籍敌侨法》的四个小时内，Mikael和其他237人就被移送了。Mari向我描述这为绑架。而且这种把在美国的人运送到国外某个阴暗监狱的举动，是我在美移民政策中从未见过的。

00:54:41.780 --> 00:54:59.420
<v Nadia Reiman>We've sent hundreds to the mega prison CECOT so far, and we're paying the Salvadoran government $6 million to hold them. It's described as a prison no one leaves alive. And it's next to impossible to talk to people who are there. Not even lawyers can.
<v Nadia Reiman>到目前为止，我们已经把数百人送到了超级监狱CECOT，我们正在向萨尔瓦多政府支付600万美元来关押他们。它被描述为一座没有人能活着出来的监狱。而且与那里的人交谈是不可能的。连律师都不行。

00:54:59.420 --> 00:55:06.770
<v Nadia Reiman>The government is saying Mikael was sent there because he's a gang member, Tren de Aragua. So is he?
<v Nadia Reiman>政府称Mikael被送往那里是因为他是帮派成员，Tren de Aragua。那么他是吗？

00:55:06.770 --> 00:55:29.060
<v Nadia Reiman>The New York Times and CBS have made these lists with all the men taken there, looked into whether they could find any criminal activity here in the US, in Venezuela, and in surrounding countries. And so far, it looks like almost 90% either have no record or have been charged with minor offenses, like driving with a suspended license or trespassing.
<v Nadia Reiman>《纽约时报》和CBS列出了所有被带到那里的男性的名单，调查了他们是否能在美国、委内瑞拉及周边国家找到任何犯罪活动。到目前为止，看起来近90%的人要么没有犯罪记录，要么只被指控犯有轻微罪行，如无照驾驶或擅自闯入。

00:55:29.060 --> 00:55:41.990
<v Nadia Reiman>I talked to a professor named Dorothy Kronick, who's making one of these lists. She studies crime and policing in Venezuela. I asked her specifically about Mikael. She hasn't found anything suspicious.
<v Nadia Reiman>我采访了一位名叫Dorothy Kronick的教授，她正在制作这些名单之一。她研究委内瑞拉的犯罪和治安。我特别问了她关于Mikael的情况。她没发现任何可疑之处。

00:55:41.990 --> 00:55:50.910
<v Nadia Reiman>I also asked a local reporter in his hometown, this small island off the coast of Venezuela. She told me that there is no Tren de Aragua activity there.
<v Nadia Reiman>我还问了他家乡当地的一名记者，那是委内瑞拉海岸外的一个小岛。她告诉我那里没有Tren de Aragua活动。

00:55:50.910 --> 00:55:59.900
<v Nadia Reiman>Mikael lived in Colombia for a while. I did a criminal record search there, using his Venezuelan government ID number. Nothing.
<v Nadia Reiman>Mikael在哥伦比亚待过一段时间。我使用他的委内瑞拉政府身份证号码在那里进行了犯罪记录搜索。什么都没有。

00:55:59.900 --> 00:56:37.010
<v Nadia Reiman>This lack of information connecting Mikael to Tren de Aragua, that doesn't seem to deter our government. Robert Cerna, an acting field office director for ICE's removal operations, said something that stuck with me. Quote, "The lack of specific information about each individual actually highlights the risk they pose. It demonstrates that they are terrorists, with regard to whom we lack a complete profile." So according to the government, no proof that you're a criminal is the greatest proof of all.
<v Nadia Reiman>这种缺乏连接Mikael和Tren de Aragua的信息的情况，似乎并没有阻止我们的政府。ICE移送行动的代理现场办公室主任Robert Cerna说了一句让我印象深刻的话。引用：“缺乏关于每个人的具体信息实际上凸显了他们构成的风险。这表明他们是恐怖分子，关于他们我们缺乏完整的资料。”所以根据政府的说法，没有证据表明你是罪犯就是最伟大的证据。

00:56:37.010 --> 00:57:03.950
<v Nadia Reiman>Mari is convinced that Mikael's tattoos, that's the only reason the government thinks he's a gang member, which is not crazy. The government is looking at tattoos. They have a list of Tren de Aragua suspicious tattoos, and it is broad-- trains, crowns, roses, clocks, star, a Michael Jordan. If this is a list of gang tattoos, everyone under 50 is in a gang.
<v Nadia Reiman>Mari确信Mikael的纹身，那是政府认为他是帮派成员的唯一原因，这并不疯狂。政府正在看纹身。他们有一份Tren de Aragua可疑纹身名单，而且范围很广——火车、皇冠、玫瑰、时钟、星星、迈克尔·乔丹。如果这是一份帮派纹身名单，那50岁以下的人都在帮派里。

00:57:03.950 --> 00:57:34.850
<v Nadia Reiman>Ronna Risquez, a Venezuelan reporter who has been following Tren de Aragua for 10 years and wrote the book about them, told me two things. One, as far as she knows, there are no Tren de Aragua specific tattoos, so basing anything on tattoos is ridiculous. To be fair, the government says they're relying on more than just tattoos, but it's unclear in a lot of cases what else. And two, Ronna confirmed what the local reporter said, that the place Mikael is from has no Tren de Aragua activity at all.
<v Nadia Reiman>Ronna Risquez，一位关注Tren de Aragua 10年并写了关于他们的书的委内瑞拉记者，告诉了我两件事。一，据她所知，没有Tren de Aragua特定的纹身，所以基于纹身做任何判断都是荒谬的。平心而论，政府说他们依赖的不止是纹身，但在许多情况下，还不清楚其他是什么。二，Ronna证实了当地记者所说的，即Mikael来自的地方根本没有Tren de Aragua活动。

00:57:34.850 --> 00:57:42.920
<v Nadia Reiman>Mari scoffs at the idea that Mikael is some secret Tren de Aragua operative who could hide from the biggest snoop of all, her.
<v Nadia Reiman>Mari对Mikael是一个秘密的Tren de Aragua特工、可以躲过最大的“窥探者”——她，这种想法嗤之以鼻。

00:57:42.920 --> 00:57:49.700
<v Mari>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Mari>[说西班牙语]

00:57:49.700 --> 00:58:05.820
<v Interpreter>I mean, when you're with someone, you're going to notice if he's into something weird or not. And, I'm a very toxic person. Bueno, not toxic. Like, I'm not toxic when it comes to women, but I like to be on top of what he's doing all the time.
<v Interpreter>我的意思是，当你和某人在一起时，你会注意到他是否参与了奇怪的事情。而且，我是一个非常“有毒”的人。好的，不是有毒。就像，当涉及女性时我不有毒，但我喜欢一直掌握他正在做什么。

00:58:05.820 --> 00:58:24.070
<v Interpreter>I go through all his things. I go through his phone. I spent a lot of time with him at the barber shop. We were always making video calls while he was working, while I was working. It was always 24 hours a day we were in communication, we were in touch. And he's the same. He was-- he was more toxic than me.
<v Interpreter>我查看他所有的东西。我查看他的手机。我在理发店花了很多时间陪他。当他工作时，当我工作时，我们总是进行视频通话。我们一直24小时在交流，我们有联系。他也是一样。他——他比我还“有毒”。

00:58:24.070 --> 00:58:28.900
<v Mari>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Mari>[说西班牙语]

00:58:28.900 --> 00:58:45.650
<v Nadia Reiman>What Mikael is is an asylum seeker. Regardless of what his asylum claim is and whether or not you think he should get asylum, I want you to put that aside for a moment. He's in the middle of a legal process that he followed our laws to participate in.
<v Nadia Reiman>Mikael是寻求庇护者。无论他的庇护主张是什么，无论你是否认为他应该获得庇护，我想请你把那个放在一边。他正处于一个法律程序中，他遵循我们的法律参与其中。

00:58:45.650 --> 00:59:28.240
<v Nadia Reiman>I cannot overstate how crazy it is that he and others got picked up and shipped away, like we just pretended due process doesn't exist anymore. Normally, someone like Mikael would present all his evidence to a judge-- documents, testimony. And then the judge would decide if under our law Mikael could stay or if he should be deported. That's what due process is, getting to stand in court and prove who you are, where you should be. Without it, the government could send anyone to CECOT. Without due process, you have no say.
<v Nadia Reiman>我无法夸大他是如何像那样被抓走并被运走是多么疯狂，就像我们假装正当程序不再存在了一样。通常，像Mikael这样的人会向法官提交所有证据——文件、证词。然后法官会决定在我们的法律下Mikael是否能留下或是否应该被驱逐。那就是正当程序，有机会站在法庭上证明你是谁，你应该在哪里。没有它，政府可以把任何人送到CECOT。没有正当程序，你没有发言权。

00:59:28.240 --> 00:59:38.050
<v Nadia Reiman>Immediately after seeing the head shaving video, Mari gets herself together and calls for help, calls Mikael's lawyer. She's like, the lawyer will know what to do.
<v Nadia Reiman>在看到剃头视频后，Mari立即让自己振作起来并呼叫求助，打电话给Mikael的律师。她想，律师会知道该怎么办。

00:59:38.050 --> 00:59:53.020
<v Nadia Reiman>The lawyer does not know what to do. The lawyer is in shock. She told me she's never seen anything like this in over 20 years of legal practice. She can't get a hold of Mikael at all. The next day is his next hearing with the judge.
<v Nadia Reiman>律师不知道该怎么办。律师处于震惊中。她告诉我她从业20多年来从未见过这样的事情。她根本联系不到Mikael。第二天是他与法官的下一次听证会。

00:59:53.020 --> 01:00:07.750
<v Nadia Reiman>The lawyer told me that during that hearing, the judge kept calling Mikael's name. Subero? Subero? Where is Subero? The detention center didn't know. The ICE attorney didn't know. Mari was there listening in on Zoom.
<v Nadia Reiman>律师告诉我，在听证会期间，法官一直呼喊Mikael的名字。“Subero？Subero？Subero在哪里？”拘留中心不知道。ICE律师不知道。Mari在Zoom上听着。

01:00:07.750 --> 01:00:14.870
<v Mari>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Mari>[说西班牙语]

01:00:14.870 --> 01:00:31.430
<v Interpreter>They were waiting for Mikael to come on the video call that they were doing for the correctional facility where he was, which is in Pennsylvania. Mikael never came out. The cops would say nothing. The cops wouldn't give a reason. All they would say was that they were looking for him.
<v Interpreter>他们正在等待Mikael出现在他们为他所在的惩教设施进行的视频通话上，该设施在宾夕法尼亚州。Mikael从来没出来。警察什么也没说。警察不会给出理由。他们唯一会说的就是他们正在找他。

01:00:31.430 --> 01:00:34.270
<v Nadia Reiman>Whoa, so no one in the court knew where he was, either?
<v Nadia Reiman>哇，所以法庭上也没有人知道他在哪里？

01:00:34.270 --> 01:00:35.550
<v Mari>No.
<v Mari>没有。

01:00:35.550 --> 01:01:00.520
<v Nadia Reiman>Throughout this story, I felt a lot like Mari, turning to scholars, to lawyers, to think tanks that track immigration for decades to ask, what exactly is this? What's happening? And the response, over and over again, was they have no idea. Everyone is wading into new water.
<v Nadia Reiman>在整个故事中，我感觉很像Mari，求助于学者、律师、追踪移民几十年的智库，问：这到底是什么？发生了什么？而答案，一次又一次，是他们也不知道。每个人都在踏入新的水域。

01:01:00.520 --> 01:01:04.040
<v Nadia Reiman>Mari hasn't heard from Mikael in over a month.
<v Nadia Reiman>Mari已经一个多月没收到Mikael的消息了。

01:01:04.040 --> 01:01:07.130
<v Mari>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Mari>[说西班牙语]

01:01:07.130 --> 01:01:19.010
<v Interpreter>Sometimes I get to work, and I'm doing whatever, and I'm like, oh, I feel like calling Mikael to see what's going on at the barber shop. And then, obviously, I remember that he's not here anymore, that I can't do that.
<v Interpreter>有时我去工作，我在做任何事情，我想，哦，我觉得应该打电话给Mikael，看看理发店那边怎么样。然后，显然，我记得他不再在这里了，我不能那样做。

01:01:19.010 --> 01:01:22.030
<v Nadia Reiman>When was the last time a moment like that happened for you?
<v Nadia Reiman>这种时刻最后一次发生在你是什么时候？

01:01:22.030 --> 01:01:25.160
<v Mari>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Mari>[说西班牙语]

01:01:25.160 --> 01:01:54.650
<v Interpreter>Literally every day that I go to work. In the mornings, it's always the first thing that comes to my mind. Because every day when I get to work, it's the time he would call me always to say, are you OK? Did you have breakfast-- to see if I had breakfast. I always-- like, I wait for the call. I hear the phone ringing, and I'm like, it's Mikael, and like that. And that's what it's like. And it's like-- it's like the hardest thing.
<v Interpreter>字面上每天我上班时。早上，这总是我脑海中闪过的第一件事。因为每天当我到工作时，那是他总是打电话问我“你还好吗？吃过早餐了吗”的时候——看看我是否吃了早餐。我总是——就像，我等待那个电话。我听到手机响，我就想，是Mikael，就像那样。这就是那种感觉。而且它就像——就像最难的事情。

01:01:54.650 --> 01:02:00.160
<v Mari>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Mari>[说西班牙语]

01:02:00.160 --> 01:02:36.970
<v Interpreter>She can't stay in their apartment anymore because the raid was so intense. She didn't want to touch his stuff, because she kept thinking he'd be back. Some days, she says, she doesn't know what to do with herself. She feels angry all the time, tricked. Disappearing people in the middle of the night, taking them someplace where you can't see them or talk to them, that's not a US thing. She never thought something like this could happen here. Many people didn't.
<v Interpreter>她不能再呆在他们的公寓了，因为那次搜查太强烈了。她不想碰他的东西，因为她一直以为他会回来。她说有些天，她不知道该拿自己怎么办。她一直感到生气、被耍了。在深夜带走人，把他们带到你看不到或联系不到他们的地方，那不是美国的事情。她从没想过这种事情会发生在这里。许多人也没想到。

01:02:36.970 --> 01:02:52.240
